WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.460 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:04.428 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:04.428 --> 00:00:06.396 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:06.396 --> 00:00:11.330 align:middle line:90%


00:00:11.330 --> 00:00:14.000 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: All right,
why don't we start up?

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:16.435 align:middle line:84%
So we're continuing
to do syntax.

00:00:16.435 --> 00:00:17.810 align:middle line:84%
I think I promised
you that I was

00:00:17.810 --> 00:00:20.143 align:middle line:84%
going to have a look at the
syllabus, and I sort of did.

00:00:20.143 --> 00:00:22.490 align:middle line:90%
I think if--

00:00:22.490 --> 00:00:25.880 align:middle line:84%
I think we will probably
finish syntax not today,

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:27.830 align:middle line:90%
but on Thursday.

00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:29.918 align:middle line:84%
And then we will
stop doing syntax.

00:00:29.918 --> 00:00:32.210 align:middle line:84%
So if there's anyone who is
tired of syntax, I'm sorry.

00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:33.752 align:middle line:84%
Please bear with it
for another week,

00:00:33.752 --> 00:00:37.550 align:middle line:84%
and then there will be no
more syntax for a while.

00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:39.110 align:middle line:90%
This is today's "XKCD."

00:00:39.110 --> 00:00:41.882 align:middle line:84%
It's not true, as far
as I know, or maybe I'm

00:00:41.882 --> 00:00:43.340 align:middle line:84%
the only one who
didn't get a word.

00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:45.173 align:middle line:84%
That's-- I guess that's
another possibility.

00:00:45.173 --> 00:00:50.960 align:middle line:84%
We'll have to ask-- ask your
TAs in the sessions tomorrow.

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:54.038 align:middle line:90%


00:00:54.038 --> 00:01:02.560 align:middle line:84%
OK, so one topic that we keep
coming back to is the fact--

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:03.600 align:middle line:90%
seems to be a fact--

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:05.940 align:middle line:84%
that although there
are many kinds

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:08.340 align:middle line:84%
of languages in the world,
there are not as many

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:09.580 align:middle line:90%
as you could imagine.

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:12.060 align:middle line:84%
So various types of
languages, places

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:16.080 align:middle line:84%
where we've seen that languages
make choices with respect

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:19.170 align:middle line:84%
to how they do this or that
or the other syntactic thing.

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:22.440 align:middle line:84%
But you don't find all of the
choices that are logically

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:24.040 align:middle line:90%
possible.

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:26.880 align:middle line:84%
So for example, we've seen
that there are languages

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:29.310 align:middle line:84%
that move their wh-phrases
to the left periphery

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:31.740 align:middle line:84%
of the clause and there are
languages that just leave

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:33.697 align:middle line:84%
their wh-phrases where
they are, but there

00:01:33.697 --> 00:01:35.280 align:middle line:84%
don't appear to be
languages that move

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:36.867 align:middle line:90%
their wh-phrases anywhere else.

00:01:36.867 --> 00:01:39.450 align:middle line:84%
There aren't languages that move
them to the end of the clause

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:41.533 align:middle line:84%
or that put them in the
exact middle of the clause

00:01:41.533 --> 00:01:42.520 align:middle line:90%
or anything like that.

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:45.120 align:middle line:84%
So there's variation,
but not as much variation

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:47.670 align:middle line:90%
as you could imagine.

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:52.110 align:middle line:84%
I want to talk about another
way, another case of that kind,

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:56.790 align:middle line:84%
a place where there's more than
one way to build a language,

00:01:56.790 --> 00:02:01.120 align:middle line:84%
but there aren't as many
ways as there could be.

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:03.900 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to talk
about the embedded clause

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:06.330 align:middle line:84%
of this sentence, "I
thought that Mary ate sushi

00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:07.170 align:middle line:90%
with chopsticks."

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:12.090 align:middle line:84%
And actually, let's--
just as a class exercise,

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:14.910 align:middle line:84%
let's diagram that
embedded clause.

00:02:14.910 --> 00:02:16.710 align:middle line:84%
So don't worry about
the matrix clause.

00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:18.840 align:middle line:90%
So that-- what's her name?

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:23.710 align:middle line:84%
"Mary--" oh dear, I'm
going to make it future--

00:02:23.710 --> 00:02:31.450 align:middle line:84%
"will eat sushi
with chopsticks."

00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:35.020 align:middle line:90%
So let's draw a tree for this.

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:38.230 align:middle line:84%
Maybe we could start
by labeling everything.

00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:39.250 align:middle line:90%
What's "chopsticks?"

00:02:39.250 --> 00:02:40.905 align:middle line:90%
What type of word is that?

00:02:40.905 --> 00:02:41.530 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Noun.

00:02:41.530 --> 00:02:42.738 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: It's a noun.

00:02:42.738 --> 00:02:43.883 align:middle line:90%
And "with?"

00:02:43.883 --> 00:02:44.800 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Preposition.

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:45.340 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Preposition.

00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:46.480 align:middle line:90%
And "sushi?"

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:48.370 align:middle line:84%
Noun-- a particularly
delicious noun.

00:02:48.370 --> 00:02:50.290 align:middle line:90%
And "eat" is a verb.

00:02:50.290 --> 00:02:54.365 align:middle line:84%
And "will" is a tense,
and "Mary" is a noun-- one

00:02:54.365 --> 00:02:55.240 align:middle line:90%
of my favorite nouns.

00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:56.650 align:middle line:90%
And "that?"

00:02:56.650 --> 00:02:58.697 align:middle line:84%
It's a complementizer,
C, excellent.

00:02:58.697 --> 00:03:01.030 align:middle line:84%
And if anybody is sitting
there quietly thinking, "What?

00:03:01.030 --> 00:03:02.950 align:middle line:90%
Where did all that come from?"

00:03:02.950 --> 00:03:04.900 align:middle line:84%
ask your TAs, or we
can talk about it now.

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:07.510 align:middle line:84%
Is there anything on here that
people are like, wait, no.

00:03:07.510 --> 00:03:10.130 align:middle line:90%
Why did you do that?

00:03:10.130 --> 00:03:10.630 align:middle line:90%
No?

00:03:10.630 --> 00:03:11.130 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:03:11.130 --> 00:03:13.135 align:middle line:84%
All right, so now let's
begin merging things.

00:03:13.135 --> 00:03:15.670 align:middle line:90%


00:03:15.670 --> 00:03:18.340 align:middle line:84%
Somebody give me two
nodes on this tree

00:03:18.340 --> 00:03:20.444 align:middle line:90%
that I ought to merge.

00:03:20.444 --> 00:03:20.944 align:middle line:90%
Faith?

00:03:20.944 --> 00:03:22.260 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: "With"
and "chopsticks"

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:23.968 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: "With"
and "chopsticks."

00:03:23.968 --> 00:03:25.940 align:middle line:84%
And what label should
the resulting thing have?

00:03:25.940 --> 00:03:26.450 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Preposition.

00:03:26.450 --> 00:03:28.220 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah, this
is a prepositional phrase.

00:03:28.220 --> 00:03:30.470 align:middle line:84%
And because this is the
highest thing with the node N,

00:03:30.470 --> 00:03:34.430 align:middle line:84%
I'll give it a P too, so
that's now a noun phrase yeah?

00:03:34.430 --> 00:03:35.093 align:middle line:90%
Joseph?

00:03:35.093 --> 00:03:35.760 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: "Eat."

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:37.280 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
"Eat" and "sushi."

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:39.115 align:middle line:84%
And what label
should I give this?

00:03:39.115 --> 00:03:39.740 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Verb?

00:03:39.740 --> 00:03:41.060 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah, it's
going to be a V bar in the end.

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:42.390 align:middle line:90%
I'll just call it a V for now.

00:03:42.390 --> 00:03:46.320 align:middle line:84%
And now this is an NP because
its label didn't project.

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:46.820 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:48.080 align:middle line:90%
What else should I merge?

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:49.232 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: "That Mary?"

00:03:49.232 --> 00:03:50.440 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: "That Mary?"

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:52.340 align:middle line:90%
Well, I could, yeah.

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:55.434 align:middle line:90%
And what would I project there?

00:03:55.434 --> 00:03:57.103 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: A
complementizer phrase.

00:03:57.103 --> 00:03:59.645 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: This is going
to be a complementizer phrase?

00:03:59.645 --> 00:04:00.350 align:middle line:90%
OK, possibly.

00:04:00.350 --> 00:04:02.000 align:middle line:84%
And then this would
be a noun phrase.

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:04.170 align:middle line:90%
We did that, yeah.

00:04:04.170 --> 00:04:06.480 align:middle line:84%
Any other nodes here
that I ought to merge?

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:09.480 align:middle line:84%
Including nodes that we've made
in the course of doing this.

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:10.140 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:11.835 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: "Eats sushi
with chopsticks."

00:04:11.835 --> 00:04:14.252 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah, "eats
sushi" and "with chopsticks."

00:04:14.252 --> 00:04:16.079 align:middle line:90%
And what label will that have?

00:04:16.079 --> 00:04:17.470 align:middle line:90%
That's a verb phrase.

00:04:17.470 --> 00:04:17.970 align:middle line:90%
Cool.

00:04:17.970 --> 00:04:19.095 align:middle line:90%
And so now this is a V bar.

00:04:19.095 --> 00:04:21.380 align:middle line:90%
Joseph was right.

00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:24.420 align:middle line:90%
Anything else I should merge?

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:25.020 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, sorry.

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:26.596 align:middle line:90%
Go ahead, Faith."

00:04:26.596 --> 00:04:28.490 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: The two
words that should be--

00:04:28.490 --> 00:04:28.990 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
I think we should

00:04:28.990 --> 00:04:30.100 align:middle line:90%
merge these two things, yes.

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:32.730 align:middle line:84%
And we should give, as you just
said, the whole thing the label

00:04:32.730 --> 00:04:33.772 align:middle line:90%
T. Yeah, you're right.

00:04:33.772 --> 00:04:35.230 align:middle line:84%
Is that what you
were going to say?

00:04:35.230 --> 00:04:36.868 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:04:36.868 --> 00:04:37.910 align:middle line:90%
What else should I merge?

00:04:37.910 --> 00:04:38.450 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:04:38.450 --> 00:04:39.230 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Just a question.

00:04:39.230 --> 00:04:40.180 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:41.638 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Could
you quickly explain

00:04:41.638 --> 00:04:43.940 align:middle line:90%
the bars again, versus the--

00:04:43.940 --> 00:04:46.433 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh, so
here we have three nodes.

00:04:46.433 --> 00:04:47.600 align:middle line:90%
This is a nice example here.

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:49.225 align:middle line:84%
We have three nodes,
all with the label

00:04:49.225 --> 00:04:53.900 align:middle line:84%
V. P is just the name for
the highest one, yeah.

00:04:53.900 --> 00:04:56.510 align:middle line:84%
And the lowest one
doesn't get a mark,

00:04:56.510 --> 00:04:58.310 align:middle line:84%
or sometimes you'll
see people put

00:04:58.310 --> 00:05:01.910 align:middle line:84%
a raised zero just to mark the
fact that it's the lowest form.

00:05:01.910 --> 00:05:04.380 align:middle line:84%
I haven't been
bothering with that.

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:07.400 align:middle line:84%
And then everything else
is given the label bar.

00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:08.180 align:middle line:90%
It's-- yeah.

00:05:08.180 --> 00:05:09.340 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, yeah.

00:05:09.340 --> 00:05:13.760 align:middle line:84%
Just higher than zero and
lower than P. Syntactic math--

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:18.230 align:middle line:84%
we count 0, bar, P.
That's how we count.

00:05:18.230 --> 00:05:20.510 align:middle line:84%
What other things should
I merge with each other?

00:05:20.510 --> 00:05:29.130 align:middle line:90%


00:05:29.130 --> 00:05:30.832 align:middle line:90%
Does anybody remember the EPP?

00:05:30.832 --> 00:05:34.690 align:middle line:90%


00:05:34.690 --> 00:05:37.870 align:middle line:90%
One of my favorite Ps.

00:05:37.870 --> 00:05:42.507 align:middle line:84%
It says that TP must
have a specifier.

00:05:42.507 --> 00:05:46.980 align:middle line:90%


00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:52.650 align:middle line:84%
So it's responsible for the
fact that you can't just say--

00:05:52.650 --> 00:06:04.300 align:middle line:84%
you can say things like "is
obvious that syntax is fun."

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:07.480 align:middle line:84%
You put this "it"
here and this "it"

00:06:07.480 --> 00:06:09.040 align:middle line:84%
is what we were
calling an expletive.

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:12.850 align:middle line:84%
It's a meaningless thing
that you put there so

00:06:12.850 --> 00:06:14.560 align:middle line:90%
that TP can have a specifier.

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:16.810 align:middle line:84%
If I were going to draw
a tree for "it is obvious

00:06:16.810 --> 00:06:21.780 align:middle line:84%
that syntax is fun,"
it would have a TP

00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:26.130 align:middle line:84%
and "it" would be in
the specifier of that.

00:06:26.130 --> 00:06:28.690 align:middle line:84%
We'd have a verb phrase,
"is obvious," blah de blah.

00:06:28.690 --> 00:06:31.210 align:middle line:90%


00:06:31.210 --> 00:06:33.440 align:middle line:84%
So I'd have a tree
sort of like that one.

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:33.940 align:middle line:90%
Joseph?

00:06:33.940 --> 00:06:35.580 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: What
does EPP stand for?

00:06:35.580 --> 00:06:37.440 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Well,
I encouraged you not

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:38.970 align:middle line:84%
to worry about the
answer to that,

00:06:38.970 --> 00:06:42.390 align:middle line:84%
but the answer is that it
stands for "extended projection

00:06:42.390 --> 00:06:44.890 align:middle line:90%
principle."

00:06:44.890 --> 00:06:46.980 align:middle line:84%
And it is also a
parameter-- sorry,

00:06:46.980 --> 00:06:49.560 align:middle line:84%
let me see if I can spell
"principle" correctly.

00:06:49.560 --> 00:06:51.535 align:middle line:84%
It is also a parameter
in that English has it--

00:06:51.535 --> 00:06:53.910 align:middle line:84%
and there are other languages
that have it, like French--

00:06:53.910 --> 00:06:55.493 align:middle line:84%
but it's actually
not all that common.

00:06:55.493 --> 00:06:59.860 align:middle line:84%
There are plenty of languages
out there that don't have this.

00:06:59.860 --> 00:07:03.870 align:middle line:84%
So there's a general principle
that TP must have a specifier.

00:07:03.870 --> 00:07:06.900 align:middle line:84%
That is, there must be a
structure like this one

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:10.590 align:middle line:84%
where there's a TP that has,
as its daughters, something--

00:07:10.590 --> 00:07:12.090 align:middle line:90%
it's usually a noun phrase--

00:07:12.090 --> 00:07:14.600 align:middle line:90%
and then a T bar.

00:07:14.600 --> 00:07:17.960 align:middle line:90%
So we're not yet there.

00:07:17.960 --> 00:07:20.900 align:middle line:84%
This TP doesn't have a
specifier, so it needs one.

00:07:20.900 --> 00:07:25.150 align:middle line:90%


00:07:25.150 --> 00:07:25.750 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:07:25.750 --> 00:07:29.500 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Can "that"
be a specifier?

00:07:29.500 --> 00:07:31.540 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
So in a sentence

00:07:31.540 --> 00:07:37.310 align:middle line:84%
where a CP is the
subject of a clause--

00:07:37.310 --> 00:07:41.560 align:middle line:84%
so in a sentence
like "that syntax

00:07:41.560 --> 00:07:49.720 align:middle line:84%
is fun is obvious," where
the subject of the predicate

00:07:49.720 --> 00:07:52.690 align:middle line:84%
"is obvious" is this
CP, "that syntax

00:07:52.690 --> 00:07:57.100 align:middle line:84%
is fun," where "that"
is its head, then yeah.

00:07:57.100 --> 00:08:00.610 align:middle line:84%
So if this were a
clause, then yeah,

00:08:00.610 --> 00:08:02.860 align:middle line:90%
it could be the specifier of TP.

00:08:02.860 --> 00:08:03.475 align:middle line:90%
Joseph?

00:08:03.475 --> 00:08:05.920 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Could "Mary"
not merge with the T bar,

00:08:05.920 --> 00:08:08.535 align:middle line:90%
and then T merges with--

00:08:08.535 --> 00:08:09.910 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
I think we might

00:08:09.910 --> 00:08:11.493 align:middle line:84%
want to think about
it that way, yeah.

00:08:11.493 --> 00:08:13.990 align:middle line:84%
We might want to make
"Mary" the specifier of TP,

00:08:13.990 --> 00:08:15.970 align:middle line:90%
satisfying the EPP, yeah.

00:08:15.970 --> 00:08:19.750 align:middle line:84%
And then all we've
got left is a C,

00:08:19.750 --> 00:08:26.500 align:middle line:84%
and we can make that C the
sister of this TP, yeah.

00:08:26.500 --> 00:08:31.173 align:middle line:84%
Is that a tree that people
are not too unnerved by?

00:08:31.173 --> 00:08:32.590 align:middle line:84%
All of you have
all the nerves you

00:08:32.590 --> 00:08:35.510 align:middle line:90%
had before you looked at it.

00:08:35.510 --> 00:08:36.240 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:36.740 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:08:36.740 --> 00:08:42.217 align:middle line:84%
All right, so there is a
tree for the embedded clause,

00:08:42.217 --> 00:08:44.300 align:middle line:84%
the boldfaced embedded
clause "that Mary ate sushi

00:08:44.300 --> 00:08:45.350 align:middle line:90%
with chopsticks."

00:08:45.350 --> 00:08:48.290 align:middle line:84%
And here is hopefully
the same tree.

00:08:48.290 --> 00:08:49.640 align:middle line:90%
Oh, I put it in a "could," yeah.

00:08:49.640 --> 00:08:54.143 align:middle line:84%
So "that Mary could eat sushi
with chopsticks," same deal.

00:08:54.143 --> 00:08:55.590 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:08:55.590 --> 00:08:56.760 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:08:56.760 --> 00:08:58.920 align:middle line:84%
Now let me call your
attention to a fact

00:08:58.920 --> 00:09:02.160 align:middle line:84%
about this tree, which some of
you may already have noticed.

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:05.160 align:middle line:84%
A general rule when you're
drawing a tree for English,

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:07.590 align:middle line:84%
that if you have a head
and the head has a sister,

00:09:07.590 --> 00:09:10.320 align:middle line:90%
the head goes before the sister.

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:13.620 align:middle line:84%
So we have prepositional
phrases like "with chopsticks"

00:09:13.620 --> 00:09:17.220 align:middle line:84%
and verb phrases that have--
if they have an object,

00:09:17.220 --> 00:09:19.590 align:middle line:84%
you get the verb before
the object, "eat sushi."

00:09:19.590 --> 00:09:22.500 align:middle line:84%
And T, like "could" or "will,"
which we have on the board,

00:09:22.500 --> 00:09:24.510 align:middle line:84%
precedes its complement,
the verb phrase.

00:09:24.510 --> 00:09:27.000 align:middle line:84%
And C, the "that"
which is up there,

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:28.810 align:middle line:84%
precedes its complement,
which is the TP.

00:09:28.810 --> 00:09:30.060 align:middle line:90%
We have all these blue arrows.

00:09:30.060 --> 00:09:31.260 align:middle line:84%
On the tree, the
blue arrows are just

00:09:31.260 --> 00:09:32.760 align:middle line:84%
meant to-- you don't have
to draw them if you're

00:09:32.760 --> 00:09:33.760 align:middle line:90%
drawing an English tree.

00:09:33.760 --> 00:09:36.563 align:middle line:84%
They're just there to
dramatically represent the fact

00:09:36.563 --> 00:09:37.980 align:middle line:84%
that here we have
all these heads,

00:09:37.980 --> 00:09:39.750 align:middle line:84%
and they're preceding
their complements.

00:09:39.750 --> 00:09:41.628 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:09:41.628 --> 00:09:43.170 align:middle line:84%
What would English
look like if heads

00:09:43.170 --> 00:09:45.030 align:middle line:90%
followed their complements?

00:09:45.030 --> 00:09:48.400 align:middle line:84%
Well, you'd get weird
orders like that one--

00:09:48.400 --> 00:09:51.230 align:middle line:84%
"Mary chopsticks with
sushi eat could that,"

00:09:51.230 --> 00:09:52.105 align:middle line:90%
which is not English.

00:09:52.105 --> 00:09:54.910 align:middle line:90%


00:09:54.910 --> 00:09:57.190 align:middle line:90%
But it is Japanese.

00:09:57.190 --> 00:10:01.810 align:middle line:84%
So in Japanese, the way you
say "that Mary could eat sushi

00:10:01.810 --> 00:10:03.940 align:middle line:84%
with chopsticks" is
literally something

00:10:03.940 --> 00:10:07.930 align:middle line:84%
like "Mary chopsticks
with sushi eat good that."

00:10:07.930 --> 00:10:10.000 align:middle line:84%
If any of you have
studied Japanese

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:11.830 align:middle line:84%
or are thinking about
studying Japanese,

00:10:11.830 --> 00:10:13.210 align:middle line:84%
be aware that this
is something you'll

00:10:13.210 --> 00:10:15.252 align:middle line:84%
have to learn to cope
with, saying your sentences

00:10:15.252 --> 00:10:17.110 align:middle line:90%
in this different order.

00:10:17.110 --> 00:10:19.150 align:middle line:90%
So you say, [SPEAKING JAPANESE].

00:10:19.150 --> 00:10:22.180 align:middle line:90%


00:10:22.180 --> 00:10:24.550 align:middle line:84%
And not just Japanese,
but well, lots

00:10:24.550 --> 00:10:26.000 align:middle line:90%
of other languages as well.

00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:29.200 align:middle line:84%
So that's the basic word order
for Tibetan and for Korean

00:10:29.200 --> 00:10:31.103 align:middle line:84%
and for Navajo and
Basque and Chaha

00:10:31.103 --> 00:10:32.770 align:middle line:84%
and a zillion other
languages out there.

00:10:32.770 --> 00:10:34.978 align:middle line:84%
It's, cross linguistically,
a very common word order.

00:10:34.978 --> 00:10:37.055 align:middle line:84%
In fact, if you just
count languages,

00:10:37.055 --> 00:10:38.680 align:middle line:84%
it might be the most
common word order.

00:10:38.680 --> 00:10:44.280 align:middle line:84%
It's slightly more common than
the English style word order.

00:10:44.280 --> 00:10:50.070 align:middle line:84%
OK, so here's a single switch
that you can flick, right?

00:10:50.070 --> 00:10:51.960 align:middle line:84%
Do your heads precede
your complements

00:10:51.960 --> 00:10:53.550 align:middle line:84%
or do they follow
your complements?

00:10:53.550 --> 00:10:56.130 align:middle line:84%
So in English, the heads
precede the complements.

00:10:56.130 --> 00:10:58.170 align:middle line:84%
In Japanese and lots
of other languages,

00:10:58.170 --> 00:11:00.262 align:middle line:90%
they follow the complements.

00:11:00.262 --> 00:11:02.220 align:middle line:84%
There's one basic difference
between languages.

00:11:02.220 --> 00:11:04.880 align:middle line:90%


00:11:04.880 --> 00:11:12.170 align:middle line:84%
Now it used to be that this was
the point in the class where

00:11:12.170 --> 00:11:14.357 align:middle line:84%
I would tell you that,
and I would wait just

00:11:14.357 --> 00:11:16.190 align:middle line:84%
long enough for you to
be impressed by that,

00:11:16.190 --> 00:11:18.080 align:middle line:84%
and then I would quickly
change the subject

00:11:18.080 --> 00:11:21.170 align:middle line:84%
and hope that none
of you spoke German.

00:11:21.170 --> 00:11:23.180 align:middle line:84%
Because there are
languages out there,

00:11:23.180 --> 00:11:25.650 align:middle line:84%
sadly, in which some heads
are initial and other heads

00:11:25.650 --> 00:11:26.150 align:middle line:90%
are final.

00:11:26.150 --> 00:11:28.010 align:middle line:84%
That is, some heads precede
their complements and others

00:11:28.010 --> 00:11:29.420 align:middle line:90%
follow their complements.

00:11:29.420 --> 00:11:31.770 align:middle line:90%
German is such a language.

00:11:31.770 --> 00:11:36.080 align:middle line:84%
So for example, languages with
mixed headedness-- in German,

00:11:36.080 --> 00:11:39.830 align:middle line:84%
here's German for "that
Mary could eat sushi

00:11:39.830 --> 00:11:40.550 align:middle line:90%
with chopsticks."

00:11:40.550 --> 00:11:43.660 align:middle line:84%
And you can see the German
complementizer precedes

00:11:43.660 --> 00:11:44.160 align:middle line:90%
the clause.

00:11:44.160 --> 00:11:46.220 align:middle line:84%
So the German compromiser
for that is "dass,"

00:11:46.220 --> 00:11:49.430 align:middle line:84%
and it goes before the
clause, just like in English.

00:11:49.430 --> 00:11:52.930 align:middle line:84%
And German has prepositions,
so "with chopsticks"

00:11:52.930 --> 00:11:55.370 align:middle line:90%
is "mit Stäbchen."

00:11:55.370 --> 00:11:56.920 align:middle line:84%
So "with" goes
before "chopsticks."

00:11:56.920 --> 00:11:58.670 align:middle line:84%
You know, German has
prepositional phrases

00:11:58.670 --> 00:12:00.140 align:middle line:90%
just like English.

00:12:00.140 --> 00:12:03.710 align:middle line:84%
But German verbs and
German tense, whatever

00:12:03.710 --> 00:12:05.840 align:middle line:84%
we put in T, these
kinds of auxiliaries,

00:12:05.840 --> 00:12:07.340 align:middle line:84%
at least in this
kind of clause--

00:12:07.340 --> 00:12:08.630 align:middle line:90%
we'll come back to this--

00:12:08.630 --> 00:12:10.580 align:middle line:84%
they come after
their complements.

00:12:10.580 --> 00:12:13.465 align:middle line:84%
So if you're saying
in German, "I

00:12:13.465 --> 00:12:15.590 align:middle line:84%
thought that Mary could
eat sushi with chopsticks,"

00:12:15.590 --> 00:12:16.970 align:middle line:84%
the word order is
literally going

00:12:16.970 --> 00:12:21.170 align:middle line:84%
to be something like "I thought
that Mary with chopsticks sushi

00:12:21.170 --> 00:12:23.270 align:middle line:90%
eat could."

00:12:23.270 --> 00:12:27.680 align:middle line:84%
Mark Twain has a great essay
called "The Awful German

00:12:27.680 --> 00:12:34.280 align:middle line:84%
Language" in which he says many
hilariously partially accurate

00:12:34.280 --> 00:12:38.480 align:middle line:84%
things about German, one of them
being that-- he says something

00:12:38.480 --> 00:12:41.870 align:middle line:84%
like, when a German dives into
a sentence, that is the last you

00:12:41.870 --> 00:12:45.170 align:middle line:84%
will see of him until he
emerges from the other side

00:12:45.170 --> 00:12:47.900 align:middle line:84%
of the Atlantic with
his verb in his mouth.

00:12:47.900 --> 00:12:49.580 align:middle line:84%
Because he's making
fun of the fact

00:12:49.580 --> 00:12:52.340 align:middle line:84%
that German often has the verb
at the end of the sentence,

00:12:52.340 --> 00:12:55.640 align:middle line:84%
and goes on and
on about how it's

00:12:55.640 --> 00:12:58.590 align:middle line:84%
very easy to forget what exactly
is being done to these things.

00:12:58.590 --> 00:13:00.440 align:middle line:84%
They're just being
named, and you find out

00:13:00.440 --> 00:13:03.210 align:middle line:90%
the verb weeks later.

00:13:03.210 --> 00:13:03.790 align:middle line:90%
So, yeah.

00:13:03.790 --> 00:13:06.290 align:middle line:84%
So in English, heads precede
their complements; in Japanese,

00:13:06.290 --> 00:13:09.190 align:middle line:84%
heads follow their complements
And in other languages,

00:13:09.190 --> 00:13:11.915 align:middle line:84%
some other languages like
German, you get both.

00:13:11.915 --> 00:13:13.790 align:middle line:84%
There are some heads
that precede complements

00:13:13.790 --> 00:13:16.850 align:middle line:84%
and others that follow
their complements, OK?

00:13:16.850 --> 00:13:17.620 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, Faith.

00:13:17.620 --> 00:13:19.795 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is there any rule
for which heads precede

00:13:19.795 --> 00:13:20.530 align:middle line:90%
and which follow?

00:13:20.530 --> 00:13:21.070 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Ah, good.

00:13:21.070 --> 00:13:21.940 align:middle line:90%
Good question.

00:13:21.940 --> 00:13:23.560 align:middle line:84%
That was the next
question I was going

00:13:23.560 --> 00:13:24.820 align:middle line:90%
to ask myself, rhetorically.

00:13:24.820 --> 00:13:27.580 align:middle line:84%
Thank you for asking
me non-rhetorically.

00:13:27.580 --> 00:13:31.420 align:middle line:84%
You might wonder, OK,
fine, so do we just

00:13:31.420 --> 00:13:33.370 align:middle line:84%
have to say, for every
head in every language,

00:13:33.370 --> 00:13:35.140 align:middle line:84%
this head precedes,
this head follows?

00:13:35.140 --> 00:13:36.130 align:middle line:90%
Is there any rule?

00:13:36.130 --> 00:13:38.500 align:middle line:84%
Do we get to say anything
more interesting than that?

00:13:38.500 --> 00:13:40.270 align:middle line:84%
It turns out that
there are kinds

00:13:40.270 --> 00:13:46.310 align:middle line:84%
of systems that don't exist,
which is kind of interesting.

00:13:46.310 --> 00:13:51.820 align:middle line:84%
So let's concentrate
on the heads T and V.

00:13:51.820 --> 00:13:54.370 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to look
at embedded clauses

00:13:54.370 --> 00:13:56.830 align:middle line:84%
like this one, where you've
got something in tense,

00:13:56.830 --> 00:13:59.080 align:middle line:84%
an auxiliary of some kind,
and you've got a verb,

00:13:59.080 --> 00:14:00.910 align:middle line:90%
and there's also an object.

00:14:00.910 --> 00:14:04.450 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to look at the
ordering of those two heads,

00:14:04.450 --> 00:14:06.430 align:middle line:84%
T with respect to
the verb phrase,

00:14:06.430 --> 00:14:09.340 align:middle line:84%
and the verb with
respect to the object.

00:14:09.340 --> 00:14:12.330 align:middle line:84%
If you do that,
here's what you find.

00:14:12.330 --> 00:14:15.930 align:middle line:84%
You get languages, like
English, in which "has"

00:14:15.930 --> 00:14:18.720 align:middle line:84%
and "read" both precede
their complements.

00:14:18.720 --> 00:14:22.840 align:middle line:84%
You get languages like German,
in which "read" and "has"

00:14:22.840 --> 00:14:24.090 align:middle line:90%
both follow their complements.

00:14:24.090 --> 00:14:26.760 align:middle line:90%
That's why they're both blue.

00:14:26.760 --> 00:14:29.640 align:middle line:84%
You get languages
like West Flemish,

00:14:29.640 --> 00:14:33.240 align:middle line:84%
which is a language
closely related to Dutch--

00:14:33.240 --> 00:14:35.482 align:middle line:90%
spoken, I assume, in West Flem--

00:14:35.482 --> 00:14:36.720 align:middle line:90%
[STUDENT SNEEZING]

00:14:36.720 --> 00:14:39.180 align:middle line:84%
--in which the
auxiliary-- bless you--

00:14:39.180 --> 00:14:42.150 align:middle line:84%
the auxiliary precedes the
verb phrase, and in which

00:14:42.150 --> 00:14:43.990 align:middle line:90%
the verb follows the object.

00:14:43.990 --> 00:14:47.460 align:middle line:84%
So in West Flemish, you say that
"John wants a house to buy."

00:14:47.460 --> 00:14:50.700 align:middle line:90%


00:14:50.700 --> 00:14:51.690 align:middle line:90%
I wasn't able to do--

00:14:51.690 --> 00:14:53.160 align:middle line:84%
I don't speak West
Flemish, so I wasn't

00:14:53.160 --> 00:14:54.780 align:middle line:84%
able to do "that John
has read a book."

00:14:54.780 --> 00:14:56.697 align:middle line:84%
I guess I should make
all of these "John wants

00:14:56.697 --> 00:14:58.180 align:middle line:90%
to buy a house."

00:14:58.180 --> 00:15:00.900 align:middle line:84%
So OK, so you get languages like
English, in which both of those

00:15:00.900 --> 00:15:03.997 align:middle line:84%
precede their complements,
both the T and the V

00:15:03.997 --> 00:15:05.080 align:middle line:90%
precede their complements.

00:15:05.080 --> 00:15:07.230 align:middle line:84%
You get languages like German,
in which they both follow.

00:15:07.230 --> 00:15:08.730 align:middle line:84%
You get languages
like West Flemish,

00:15:08.730 --> 00:15:13.390 align:middle line:84%
in which the auxiliary
precedes and the verb follows.

00:15:13.390 --> 00:15:17.580 align:middle line:84%
But you never get the fourth
imaginable kind of language--

00:15:17.580 --> 00:15:18.510 align:middle line:90%
not ever.

00:15:18.510 --> 00:15:20.520 align:middle line:90%
People have looked quite hard.

00:15:20.520 --> 00:15:22.548 align:middle line:84%
Where I, again, am
using silly diacritics

00:15:22.548 --> 00:15:24.840 align:middle line:84%
to emphasize the fact that
this language doesn't exist.

00:15:24.840 --> 00:15:26.070 align:middle line:90%
I'm making it up, yeah.

00:15:26.070 --> 00:15:27.930 align:middle line:84%
So there are no languages
in which you say

00:15:27.930 --> 00:15:29.640 align:middle line:90%
"that John read the book has."

00:15:29.640 --> 00:15:34.300 align:middle line:84%
That's not a possible human
language, for some reason.

00:15:34.300 --> 00:15:35.968 align:middle line:84%
So we get English,
where those heads

00:15:35.968 --> 00:15:37.260 align:middle line:90%
both precede their complements.

00:15:37.260 --> 00:15:39.593 align:middle line:84%
We get German, where they
both follow their complements.

00:15:39.593 --> 00:15:43.270 align:middle line:84%
We get West Flemish, where
the lower head follows

00:15:43.270 --> 00:15:44.520 align:middle line:90%
the complement, is head final.

00:15:44.520 --> 00:15:47.820 align:middle line:84%
So the verb follows the
object, but the higher head, T,

00:15:47.820 --> 00:15:49.020 align:middle line:90%
precedes its complement.

00:15:49.020 --> 00:15:50.025 align:middle line:90%
The T is head initial.

00:15:50.025 --> 00:15:52.470 align:middle line:84%
You never get the mirror
image of West Flemish.

00:15:52.470 --> 00:15:55.470 align:middle line:90%
That doesn't happen.

00:15:55.470 --> 00:15:58.920 align:middle line:84%
It not only doesn't
happen, but it sometimes

00:15:58.920 --> 00:16:01.870 align:middle line:84%
fails to happen in kind
of interesting ways.

00:16:01.870 --> 00:16:04.110 align:middle line:90%
Here's a fact about Finnish--

00:16:04.110 --> 00:16:09.690 align:middle line:84%
Finnish word order is
mostly sort of English-like.

00:16:09.690 --> 00:16:12.030 align:middle line:84%
T and V both precede
their complements.

00:16:12.030 --> 00:16:15.600 align:middle line:84%
But just if you're asking
wh-questions, for some reason,

00:16:15.600 --> 00:16:18.220 align:middle line:84%
Finnish word order
becomes quite random.

00:16:18.220 --> 00:16:22.050 align:middle line:84%
So you can ask questions
like "When would Jussi

00:16:22.050 --> 00:16:23.040 align:middle line:90%
have written a novel?"

00:16:23.040 --> 00:16:25.650 align:middle line:84%
in the English word order,
where both of those heads

00:16:25.650 --> 00:16:28.320 align:middle line:84%
are red because they're
preceding their complements.

00:16:28.320 --> 00:16:30.480 align:middle line:84%
You can ask it in
the German word

00:16:30.480 --> 00:16:32.310 align:middle line:84%
order, where both
of those heads are

00:16:32.310 --> 00:16:33.600 align:middle line:90%
following their complements.

00:16:33.600 --> 00:16:35.730 align:middle line:84%
So you're literally
saying, "When Jussi

00:16:35.730 --> 00:16:38.490 align:middle line:90%
a novel written would have?"

00:16:38.490 --> 00:16:40.775 align:middle line:84%
You can say it in the
West Flemish order, where

00:16:40.775 --> 00:16:42.900 align:middle line:84%
the auxiliary precedes the
verb phrase and the verb

00:16:42.900 --> 00:16:44.250 align:middle line:90%
comes after the object.

00:16:44.250 --> 00:16:46.950 align:middle line:84%
But you cannot say it in the
cross linguistically unattested

00:16:46.950 --> 00:16:48.450 align:middle line:90%
order.

00:16:48.450 --> 00:16:50.820 align:middle line:84%
So it isn't just that there
are no languages like that.

00:16:50.820 --> 00:16:52.530 align:middle line:84%
Even in languages
like Finnish, where

00:16:52.530 --> 00:16:54.280 align:middle line:84%
there's a fair amount
of freedom of order,

00:16:54.280 --> 00:16:55.515 align:middle line:90%
that order is ruled out.

00:16:55.515 --> 00:16:58.390 align:middle line:90%


00:16:58.390 --> 00:16:59.920 align:middle line:90%
Which is weird.

00:16:59.920 --> 00:17:02.080 align:middle line:84%
It would be nice to
have a theory of that.

00:17:02.080 --> 00:17:06.790 align:middle line:84%
People have worked
on theories of that.

00:17:06.790 --> 00:17:09.339 align:middle line:84%
There's something called the
Final-over-Final Constraint

00:17:09.339 --> 00:17:10.180 align:middle line:90%
that's been offered.

00:17:10.180 --> 00:17:13.150 align:middle line:84%
It's called the FOFC by
people who are into it.

00:17:13.150 --> 00:17:17.119 align:middle line:84%
What the FOFC says is, at least
for certain parts of the tree,

00:17:17.119 --> 00:17:21.730 align:middle line:84%
if you have two heads,
A and B, and A has,

00:17:21.730 --> 00:17:24.490 align:middle line:84%
inside its complement,
another head, B,

00:17:24.490 --> 00:17:29.710 align:middle line:84%
then if A is head final---
if A follows its complement--

00:17:29.710 --> 00:17:32.380 align:middle line:84%
B also has to follow
its complement.

00:17:32.380 --> 00:17:34.930 align:middle line:84%
It's as though, as you're
building the tree--

00:17:34.930 --> 00:17:36.760 align:middle line:84%
we've been building
trees sort of way

00:17:36.760 --> 00:17:38.980 align:middle line:84%
we built this one,
where you start--

00:17:38.980 --> 00:17:40.450 align:middle line:84%
you're doing repeated
merge, right?

00:17:40.450 --> 00:17:41.950 align:middle line:84%
You start at the
bottom of the tree,

00:17:41.950 --> 00:17:43.780 align:middle line:84%
and you keep adding
things, and the tree

00:17:43.780 --> 00:17:44.950 align:middle line:90%
gets larger and larger.

00:17:44.950 --> 00:17:48.280 align:middle line:84%
It's as though,
at the beginning,

00:17:48.280 --> 00:17:51.130 align:middle line:84%
you decide whether your heads
are going to proceed or follow

00:17:51.130 --> 00:17:52.090 align:middle line:90%
their complements.

00:17:52.090 --> 00:17:57.430 align:middle line:84%
And then you can switch to being
head initial, but just once.

00:17:57.430 --> 00:17:59.410 align:middle line:84%
After you've decided
to be head initial,

00:17:59.410 --> 00:18:01.180 align:middle line:84%
you have to be head
initial from then on.

00:18:01.180 --> 00:18:04.150 align:middle line:84%
You can't go back to being head
final, something like that.

00:18:04.150 --> 00:18:05.342 align:middle line:90%
You can switch from being--

00:18:05.342 --> 00:18:07.300 align:middle line:84%
as you're building the
tree from the bottom up,

00:18:07.300 --> 00:18:09.400 align:middle line:84%
you can switch from being head
final to being head initial.

00:18:09.400 --> 00:18:11.055 align:middle line:84%
You can switch from
having heads follow

00:18:11.055 --> 00:18:12.430 align:middle line:84%
their complements
to having heads

00:18:12.430 --> 00:18:13.540 align:middle line:90%
precede their complements.

00:18:13.540 --> 00:18:15.220 align:middle line:84%
That's what West
Flemish does, where

00:18:15.220 --> 00:18:16.930 align:middle line:84%
it has the verb after
the object and then

00:18:16.930 --> 00:18:19.570 align:middle line:84%
it has the auxiliary
before the verb phrase.

00:18:19.570 --> 00:18:21.830 align:middle line:84%
But you can't switch
in the other direction.

00:18:21.830 --> 00:18:23.080 align:middle line:90%
That's what this seems to say.

00:18:23.080 --> 00:18:24.850 align:middle line:84%
For certain parts
of the tree-- we

00:18:24.850 --> 00:18:26.930 align:middle line:84%
may get a chance to
talk more about that.

00:18:26.930 --> 00:18:27.430 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:18:27.430 --> 00:18:29.013 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: What is
head and complement?

00:18:29.013 --> 00:18:30.138 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: I'm sorry.

00:18:30.138 --> 00:18:32.170 align:middle line:84%
By head and complement,
I just mean--

00:18:32.170 --> 00:18:37.270 align:middle line:84%
so heads are things like this,
nodes that just dominate a word

00:18:37.270 --> 00:18:39.920 align:middle line:90%
and don't contain anything else.

00:18:39.920 --> 00:18:43.330 align:middle line:84%
And the complement of
the head is its sister.

00:18:43.330 --> 00:18:49.640 align:middle line:84%
So here's a head and
here's its complement.

00:18:49.640 --> 00:18:54.880 align:middle line:84%
So this VP is the complement
of this head, or this head has,

00:18:54.880 --> 00:18:56.890 align:middle line:84%
as its complement, the
"sushi," or this head

00:18:56.890 --> 00:18:59.530 align:middle line:84%
has its complement this noun
phrase, "the chopsticks."

00:18:59.530 --> 00:19:04.252 align:middle line:84%
And the observation
is that if you have--

00:19:04.252 --> 00:19:05.850 align:middle line:84%
you can have final
heads lower down.

00:19:05.850 --> 00:19:08.350 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, you can have heads that
follow their complements lower

00:19:08.350 --> 00:19:10.767 align:middle line:84%
down and heads that precede
their complements higher up,

00:19:10.767 --> 00:19:12.100 align:middle line:90%
but you can't have the opposite.

00:19:12.100 --> 00:19:13.036 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:19:13.036 --> 00:19:15.458 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So, in
this example, we're

00:19:15.458 --> 00:19:18.820 align:middle line:90%
saying that we have phonemes.

00:19:18.820 --> 00:19:20.290 align:middle line:90%
We don't get to--

00:19:20.290 --> 00:19:21.786 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yup.

00:19:21.786 --> 00:19:24.780 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:19:24.780 --> 00:19:28.478 align:middle line:90%


00:19:28.478 --> 00:19:30.270 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So
yeah, what we're saying

00:19:30.270 --> 00:19:34.230 align:middle line:84%
is, if we go back to these
trees-- what we're saying is,

00:19:34.230 --> 00:19:39.780 align:middle line:84%
in the tree on the far
right, because the V precedes

00:19:39.780 --> 00:19:42.030 align:middle line:84%
its complement, the
higher T must also

00:19:42.030 --> 00:19:43.650 align:middle line:90%
precede its complement.

00:19:43.650 --> 00:19:46.770 align:middle line:84%
That's what the Final-over-Final
Constraint is meant to say.

00:19:46.770 --> 00:19:48.990 align:middle line:84%
If the lower head
follows the complement,

00:19:48.990 --> 00:19:51.900 align:middle line:84%
then it's OK for the higher
head to precede or follow.

00:19:51.900 --> 00:19:54.240 align:middle line:84%
But if the lower head
precedes, then the higher head

00:19:54.240 --> 00:19:55.200 align:middle line:90%
must also precede.

00:19:55.200 --> 00:19:56.900 align:middle line:90%
It cannot follow.

00:19:56.900 --> 00:19:57.900 align:middle line:90%
I hope I wrote it right.

00:19:57.900 --> 00:20:00.710 align:middle line:90%


00:20:00.710 --> 00:20:03.410 align:middle line:84%
If A follows its complement,
B must also-- wait a minute.

00:20:03.410 --> 00:20:05.540 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:20:05.540 --> 00:20:10.030 align:middle line:84%
A follows its complement, B
must also-- yes, yes, yes.

00:20:10.030 --> 00:20:14.530 align:middle line:90%
So in the trees--

00:20:14.530 --> 00:20:17.320 align:middle line:84%
in the even-numbered trees
where the higher head

00:20:17.320 --> 00:20:19.060 align:middle line:84%
follows its
complement, what we're

00:20:19.060 --> 00:20:21.230 align:middle line:84%
seeing-- where T
follows its complement.

00:20:21.230 --> 00:20:23.260 align:middle line:84%
So that's the A.
What we're seeing

00:20:23.260 --> 00:20:26.380 align:middle line:84%
is that in trees like that,
the lower head must also

00:20:26.380 --> 00:20:27.340 align:middle line:90%
follow its complement.

00:20:27.340 --> 00:20:28.723 align:middle line:90%
It can't precede.

00:20:28.723 --> 00:20:30.640 align:middle line:84%
So that's what the
Final-over-Final constraint

00:20:30.640 --> 00:20:31.920 align:middle line:90%
says.

00:20:31.920 --> 00:20:33.960 align:middle line:84%
It's an unfortunately-named
constraint.

00:20:33.960 --> 00:20:37.560 align:middle line:84%
It's misleading, but that's
what it-- that's what it does.

00:20:37.560 --> 00:20:38.060 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:20:38.060 --> 00:20:39.727 align:middle line:84%
So we get that kind
of mixed headedness,

00:20:39.727 --> 00:20:42.520 align:middle line:84%
the West Flemish kind, but
not the kind on the right.

00:20:42.520 --> 00:20:48.340 align:middle line:90%


00:20:48.340 --> 00:20:54.250 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so-- oh, I mixed
up my terminology here.

00:20:54.250 --> 00:20:54.750 align:middle line:90%
I'm sorry.

00:20:54.750 --> 00:20:56.550 align:middle line:84%
I'll fix this slide
before I post it.

00:20:56.550 --> 00:20:58.580 align:middle line:90%
So this is the FOFC violation.

00:20:58.580 --> 00:21:00.950 align:middle line:84%
T-- I called it I, which
I shouldn't have done.

00:21:00.950 --> 00:21:02.510 align:middle line:90%
That's an older word for TP.

00:21:02.510 --> 00:21:05.540 align:middle line:84%
T has V in its complement,
and T follows VP,

00:21:05.540 --> 00:21:07.220 align:middle line:90%
but V is not following NP.

00:21:07.220 --> 00:21:08.990 align:middle line:84%
That's what makes that
a Final-over-Final

00:21:08.990 --> 00:21:10.840 align:middle line:90%
Concern violation.

00:21:10.840 --> 00:21:11.340 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:21:11.340 --> 00:21:14.690 align:middle line:90%


00:21:14.690 --> 00:21:16.470 align:middle line:90%
For certain parts of the tree.

00:21:16.470 --> 00:21:18.260 align:middle line:84%
Now, for certain
parts of the tree,

00:21:18.260 --> 00:21:20.360 align:middle line:84%
there have to be restrictions
on how this applies.

00:21:20.360 --> 00:21:24.530 align:middle line:84%
So German, for
example, we've just

00:21:24.530 --> 00:21:29.960 align:middle line:84%
said that if the
higher heads are final,

00:21:29.960 --> 00:21:32.450 align:middle line:84%
the lower head also
has to be final.

00:21:32.450 --> 00:21:36.260 align:middle line:84%
So the fact that the T is
final means that the V also

00:21:36.260 --> 00:21:37.250 align:middle line:90%
has to be final.

00:21:37.250 --> 00:21:38.450 align:middle line:90%
So far, so good.

00:21:38.450 --> 00:21:42.500 align:middle line:84%
But the P is head initial,
so the people who do the FOFC

00:21:42.500 --> 00:21:44.540 align:middle line:84%
have to talk about
domains of the tree

00:21:44.540 --> 00:21:46.220 align:middle line:90%
in which the FOFC applies.

00:21:46.220 --> 00:21:49.550 align:middle line:84%
It applies to the relationship
between T and the verb,

00:21:49.550 --> 00:21:51.950 align:middle line:84%
but not to the relationship
between, say, the verb

00:21:51.950 --> 00:21:53.390 align:middle line:90%
and the prepositional phrase--

00:21:53.390 --> 00:21:54.470 align:middle line:90%
prepositional phrases.

00:21:54.470 --> 00:21:58.070 align:middle line:84%
It's like they talk as though--
the FOFC kind of starts over

00:21:58.070 --> 00:21:58.970 align:middle line:90%
at certain points.

00:21:58.970 --> 00:22:01.648 align:middle line:84%
So you calculate it within
the prepositional phrase,

00:22:01.648 --> 00:22:03.440 align:middle line:84%
or you calculate it
within the noun phrase,

00:22:03.440 --> 00:22:05.485 align:middle line:90%
but then you start again.

00:22:05.485 --> 00:22:06.860 align:middle line:84%
There are other
parts of the tree

00:22:06.860 --> 00:22:10.958 align:middle line:84%
to which the FOFC applies, but
not across those boundaries.

00:22:10.958 --> 00:22:13.250 align:middle line:84%
Lots of work on trying to
figure out why that would be.

00:22:13.250 --> 00:22:16.190 align:middle line:84%
Lots of questions about the
FOFC, like why is it true

00:22:16.190 --> 00:22:19.520 align:middle line:84%
and which chunks of
the tree is it true in?

00:22:19.520 --> 00:22:21.860 align:middle line:84%
But I'm telling you
about it because it

00:22:21.860 --> 00:22:23.990 align:middle line:84%
looks like another case
of a linguistic universal,

00:22:23.990 --> 00:22:26.840 align:middle line:84%
so another place
where, yes, there's

00:22:26.840 --> 00:22:28.370 align:middle line:90%
more than one kind of language.

00:22:28.370 --> 00:22:30.650 align:middle line:84%
There are head initial
languages like English.

00:22:30.650 --> 00:22:33.075 align:middle line:84%
There are head final
languages like Japanese.

00:22:33.075 --> 00:22:35.700 align:middle line:84%
There are languages like German,
which have some initial heads,

00:22:35.700 --> 00:22:36.650 align:middle line:90%
and other final heads.

00:22:36.650 --> 00:22:39.260 align:middle line:84%
But you don't get every
logically possible combination

00:22:39.260 --> 00:22:40.730 align:middle line:90%
of initial and final.

00:22:40.730 --> 00:22:42.985 align:middle line:90%
That's the result of this.

00:22:42.985 --> 00:22:44.610 align:middle line:84%
And the FOFC is meant
to describe that.

00:22:44.610 --> 00:22:47.027 align:middle line:84%
And then, of course, we want
to know why the FOFC is true,

00:22:47.027 --> 00:22:50.180 align:middle line:90%
if it turns out to be true.

00:22:50.180 --> 00:22:52.398 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:22:52.398 --> 00:22:54.158 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:22:54.158 --> 00:22:54.950 align:middle line:90%
Does it make sense?

00:22:54.950 --> 00:22:56.325 align:middle line:84%
Do we have a
question about this?

00:22:56.325 --> 00:22:56.952 align:middle line:90%
FOFC?

00:22:56.952 --> 00:22:58.160 align:middle line:90%
I'm sorry, I called you FOFC.

00:22:58.160 --> 00:22:58.660 align:middle line:90%
Faith?

00:22:58.660 --> 00:23:01.130 align:middle line:90%


00:23:01.130 --> 00:23:03.160 align:middle line:90%
I will stop calling you FOFC.

00:23:03.160 --> 00:23:06.020 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Just a question
about linguistic universals.

00:23:06.020 --> 00:23:08.587 align:middle line:84%
So is it always that
something doesn't exist?

00:23:08.587 --> 00:23:10.170 align:middle line:84%
Or are there any
linguistic universals

00:23:10.170 --> 00:23:13.130 align:middle line:84%
where we can say, like, oh, this
is the case in every language?

00:23:13.130 --> 00:23:15.800 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh, oh, oh.

00:23:15.800 --> 00:23:18.230 align:middle line:84%
I mean, sometimes
this is just a matter

00:23:18.230 --> 00:23:20.550 align:middle line:84%
of stating the universals
in a particular way.

00:23:20.550 --> 00:23:25.070 align:middle line:84%
So every language
has the property

00:23:25.070 --> 00:23:26.390 align:middle line:90%
that it obeys the FOFC.

00:23:26.390 --> 00:23:28.262 align:middle line:90%
There.

00:23:28.262 --> 00:23:30.180 align:middle line:84%
There are some other
things sort of like that.

00:23:30.180 --> 00:23:33.680 align:middle line:84%
So for example, it is
true of every language

00:23:33.680 --> 00:23:36.560 align:middle line:84%
in which the verb comes at
the beginning of the clause

00:23:36.560 --> 00:23:41.600 align:middle line:84%
that wh-movement happens
overtly, at least optionally.

00:23:41.600 --> 00:23:44.960 align:middle line:84%
So we said there are languages
that do overt wh-movement.

00:23:44.960 --> 00:23:46.760 align:middle line:84%
There are languages
that have wh-in-situ.

00:23:46.760 --> 00:23:49.530 align:middle line:84%
There are languages
that have both options.

00:23:49.530 --> 00:23:51.385 align:middle line:84%
And there are verb
initial languages

00:23:51.385 --> 00:23:52.760 align:middle line:84%
that have both
options, and there

00:23:52.760 --> 00:23:54.927 align:middle line:84%
are verb initial languages
that have overt movement.

00:23:54.927 --> 00:23:56.390 align:middle line:84%
There are no verb
initial languages

00:23:56.390 --> 00:23:58.070 align:middle line:90%
with obligatory wh-in-situ.

00:23:58.070 --> 00:23:59.010 align:middle line:90%
That never happens.

00:23:59.010 --> 00:24:02.873 align:middle line:84%
And again, we want to understand
why, but that's a fact.

00:24:02.873 --> 00:24:04.290 align:middle line:84%
These parts where
I keep saying we

00:24:04.290 --> 00:24:07.860 align:middle line:84%
want to understand why, that's
because there is work on this.

00:24:07.860 --> 00:24:10.140 align:middle line:84%
I have a book that tries
to derive that fact I just

00:24:10.140 --> 00:24:11.430 align:middle line:90%
told you about.

00:24:11.430 --> 00:24:15.540 align:middle line:84%
But I feel-- I would
feel bad teaching you

00:24:15.540 --> 00:24:17.760 align:middle line:84%
theories that I have
posited in a book

00:24:17.760 --> 00:24:19.770 align:middle line:90%
because I could be wrong.

00:24:19.770 --> 00:24:22.200 align:middle line:84%
I know, it's hard to
believe, but I could be.

00:24:22.200 --> 00:24:24.450 align:middle line:84%
I'm only trying to tell you
things that are definitely

00:24:24.450 --> 00:24:27.000 align:middle line:90%
true in this class.

00:24:27.000 --> 00:24:27.700 align:middle line:90%
Good question.

00:24:27.700 --> 00:24:30.150 align:middle line:90%
Are there other questions?

00:24:30.150 --> 00:24:31.420 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:24:31.420 --> 00:24:37.470 align:middle line:84%
All right, so that
was it for German.

00:24:37.470 --> 00:24:38.310 align:middle line:90%
Oh right, sorry.

00:24:38.310 --> 00:24:40.650 align:middle line:90%
Back to German.

00:24:40.650 --> 00:24:42.180 align:middle line:90%
OK, so here we are doing German.

00:24:42.180 --> 00:24:46.900 align:middle line:90%
Now I said--

00:24:46.900 --> 00:24:50.460 align:middle line:84%
I think I said this accurately,
that in this kind of clause,

00:24:50.460 --> 00:24:53.520 align:middle line:84%
in German, the verb
comes after the object,

00:24:53.520 --> 00:24:56.410 align:middle line:84%
and tense comes after
the verb phrase.

00:24:56.410 --> 00:24:59.430 align:middle line:84%
And I think I saw people
who know some German furrow

00:24:59.430 --> 00:25:02.220 align:middle line:84%
their brows at me because
that's not the word

00:25:02.220 --> 00:25:04.020 align:middle line:90%
order in a German main clause.

00:25:04.020 --> 00:25:05.520 align:middle line:84%
So I want to talk
now about the word

00:25:05.520 --> 00:25:07.980 align:middle line:90%
order in a German main clause.

00:25:07.980 --> 00:25:09.960 align:middle line:84%
Here's the word order
in a German main clause.

00:25:09.960 --> 00:25:13.020 align:middle line:84%
You say in German
things like "Mary could

00:25:13.020 --> 00:25:15.700 align:middle line:90%
with chopsticks sushi eat."

00:25:15.700 --> 00:25:18.420 align:middle line:84%
So the verb is still
after its object,

00:25:18.420 --> 00:25:20.610 align:middle line:90%
but T is no longer at the end.

00:25:20.610 --> 00:25:22.170 align:middle line:90%
It's now, well, earlier.

00:25:22.170 --> 00:25:24.870 align:middle line:90%
It's right after "Mary."

00:25:24.870 --> 00:25:28.380 align:middle line:84%
This is a property of
German main clauses

00:25:28.380 --> 00:25:33.420 align:middle line:84%
that the thing that's in T, if
there is something that's in T,

00:25:33.420 --> 00:25:38.140 align:middle line:84%
has to be preceded by
exactly one phrase.

00:25:38.140 --> 00:25:41.250 align:middle line:84%
So you can say, "Mary could
with chopsticks sushi eat."

00:25:41.250 --> 00:25:45.600 align:middle line:84%
You can say, "With chopsticks
could Mary sushi eat."

00:25:45.600 --> 00:25:49.130 align:middle line:84%
You can say "Sushi could
Mary with chopsticks eat."

00:25:49.130 --> 00:25:51.810 align:middle line:84%
You get to take some
phrase and put it first.

00:25:51.810 --> 00:25:55.410 align:middle line:84%
And then what's in T--
what would be in T,

00:25:55.410 --> 00:25:58.590 align:middle line:90%
"could," that has to be second.

00:25:58.590 --> 00:26:01.320 align:middle line:90%
This is called verb second.

00:26:01.320 --> 00:26:03.618 align:middle line:84%
These are examples where
there's an auxiliary

00:26:03.618 --> 00:26:04.410 align:middle line:90%
in second position.

00:26:04.410 --> 00:26:06.077 align:middle line:84%
If there is no
auxiliary, then the thing

00:26:06.077 --> 00:26:08.590 align:middle line:84%
that goes in second
position is the verb.

00:26:08.590 --> 00:26:12.630 align:middle line:84%
So if you wanted to say "Mary
eats sushi with chopsticks,"

00:26:12.630 --> 00:26:15.245 align:middle line:84%
so there's no auxiliary, you
would take the verb "eat."

00:26:15.245 --> 00:26:16.620 align:middle line:84%
You'd put it in
a different form.

00:26:16.620 --> 00:26:19.380 align:middle line:84%
It would be [GERMAN] and
it would go in the place

00:26:19.380 --> 00:26:21.330 align:middle line:84%
where "could" is going
in these sentences.

00:26:21.330 --> 00:26:24.490 align:middle line:84%
Something else would
be in first position.

00:26:24.490 --> 00:26:26.790 align:middle line:84%
So this is a
phenomenon called V2.

00:26:26.790 --> 00:26:30.360 align:middle line:84%
It's called V2 because you
must take exactly one phrase

00:26:30.360 --> 00:26:35.160 align:middle line:84%
and put it first, and then
T or the verb goes second.

00:26:35.160 --> 00:26:37.440 align:middle line:84%
So you can say all of
these things in German,

00:26:37.440 --> 00:26:40.625 align:middle line:84%
but you cannot say, for example,
"With chopsticks sushi could

00:26:40.625 --> 00:26:42.000 align:middle line:84%
Mary eat," or
anything like that.

00:26:42.000 --> 00:26:44.042 align:middle line:84%
There can't be two phrases
in the first position.

00:26:44.042 --> 00:26:46.770 align:middle line:90%


00:26:46.770 --> 00:26:51.420 align:middle line:84%
There must be exactly one
phrase in the first position.

00:26:51.420 --> 00:26:54.260 align:middle line:84%
So verb second-- German
clauses, German main clauses--

00:26:54.260 --> 00:26:56.210 align:middle line:84%
actually, we'll refine
that in just a second.

00:26:56.210 --> 00:26:57.668 align:middle line:84%
They have to start
with exactly one

00:26:57.668 --> 00:27:00.257 align:middle line:84%
phrase followed by
the "verb," where

00:27:00.257 --> 00:27:01.340 align:middle line:90%
I've put "verb" in quotes.

00:27:01.340 --> 00:27:03.632 align:middle line:84%
Because as you've seen, it
doesn't have to be the verb.

00:27:03.632 --> 00:27:06.030 align:middle line:84%
It's actually
whatever tense is on.

00:27:06.030 --> 00:27:08.600 align:middle line:84%
So if there's an
auxiliary, then that's

00:27:08.600 --> 00:27:10.310 align:middle line:90%
the thing that goes first.

00:27:10.310 --> 00:27:13.055 align:middle line:84%
If there's no auxiliary, then
tense is realized on the verb.

00:27:13.055 --> 00:27:14.990 align:middle line:84%
The verb is pronounced
with tense morphology,

00:27:14.990 --> 00:27:17.770 align:middle line:84%
and that's what goes
in second position.

00:27:17.770 --> 00:27:19.020 align:middle line:90%
Why am I telling you all this?

00:27:19.020 --> 00:27:21.830 align:middle line:84%
Well, partly so that you can
be more fully educated people.

00:27:21.830 --> 00:27:25.870 align:middle line:84%
Now you know some things
about the grammar of German.

00:27:25.870 --> 00:27:28.420 align:middle line:90%
But actually-- oh, sorry, yes.

00:27:28.420 --> 00:27:31.690 align:middle line:84%
That's the wrong
rhetorical move to make.

00:27:31.690 --> 00:27:34.152 align:middle line:90%
Sorry, more German.

00:27:34.152 --> 00:27:36.110 align:middle line:84%
No, actually, let me
finish the rhetorical move

00:27:36.110 --> 00:27:36.730 align:middle line:90%
I was just making.

00:27:36.730 --> 00:27:39.010 align:middle line:84%
I'm telling you all this about
German partly to show you some

00:27:39.010 --> 00:27:39.850 align:middle line:90%
cool things about German.

00:27:39.850 --> 00:27:41.308 align:middle line:84%
But what we're
going to see shortly

00:27:41.308 --> 00:27:42.700 align:middle line:90%
is that this is not just German.

00:27:42.700 --> 00:27:45.800 align:middle line:84%
V2 is a cross linguistically
common phenomenon.

00:27:45.800 --> 00:27:49.330 align:middle line:84%
There are many languages
out there that are V2.

00:27:49.330 --> 00:27:52.990 align:middle line:84%
So learning this fact about--
these facts about German

00:27:52.990 --> 00:27:55.420 align:middle line:84%
will do you some good when
you are confronted with,

00:27:55.420 --> 00:27:58.810 align:middle line:84%
for example, Kashmiri or Dinka
or a number of other languages

00:27:58.810 --> 00:28:01.230 align:middle line:84%
that are out there
which are also V2.

00:28:01.230 --> 00:28:04.382 align:middle line:84%
OK, but first, I just
said German main clauses,

00:28:04.382 --> 00:28:06.340 align:middle line:84%
and then I waffled a
little bit about whether I

00:28:06.340 --> 00:28:07.540 align:middle line:90%
meant main clauses.

00:28:07.540 --> 00:28:09.730 align:middle line:84%
It isn't actually
just main clauses.

00:28:09.730 --> 00:28:13.480 align:middle line:84%
What it is is clauses that
don't have complementizers,

00:28:13.480 --> 00:28:15.400 align:middle line:84%
don't have overt
complementizers.

00:28:15.400 --> 00:28:20.590 align:middle line:84%
So main clauses are V2,
but also embedded clauses

00:28:20.590 --> 00:28:22.750 align:middle line:84%
as long as they don't
start with "dass."

00:28:22.750 --> 00:28:25.990 align:middle line:84%
So you can say there are what's
called embedded V2, where you

00:28:25.990 --> 00:28:29.120 align:middle line:84%
have an embedded clause like
the one on the German example

00:28:29.120 --> 00:28:33.730 align:middle line:84%
at the bottom of this
slide, where you're

00:28:33.730 --> 00:28:35.390 align:middle line:90%
doing the V2 phenomenon again.

00:28:35.390 --> 00:28:37.960 align:middle line:84%
And I can't remember
whether I proved that.

00:28:37.960 --> 00:28:38.860 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, so I didn't.

00:28:38.860 --> 00:28:42.010 align:middle line:84%
But you could put any phrase
in the place of "Mary."

00:28:42.010 --> 00:28:44.250 align:middle line:84%
All the shenanigans that
we did with the main clause

00:28:44.250 --> 00:28:46.750 align:middle line:84%
in the last slide, you can do
with the embedded clause here.

00:28:46.750 --> 00:28:50.792 align:middle line:84%
So he said, "Mary wanted
with chopsticks sushi eat."

00:28:50.792 --> 00:28:52.750 align:middle line:84%
You could have put "with
chopsticks" or "sushi"

00:28:52.750 --> 00:28:54.130 align:middle line:84%
in the place where
"Mary" is, and then

00:28:54.130 --> 00:28:56.047 align:middle line:84%
"Mary" would have to be
after "wanted," right?

00:28:56.047 --> 00:28:58.510 align:middle line:84%
So there's one phrase that
goes before the auxiliary

00:28:58.510 --> 00:29:03.400 align:middle line:84%
in that embedded clause,
in case of embedded V2.

00:29:03.400 --> 00:29:05.890 align:middle line:84%
What's the difference between
this type of embedded clause

00:29:05.890 --> 00:29:09.910 align:middle line:84%
and the kinds that we looked
at in the first German sentence

00:29:09.910 --> 00:29:12.010 align:middle line:84%
when we were just talking
about mixed headedness?

00:29:12.010 --> 00:29:14.385 align:middle line:84%
Well, it has to do with whether
there's a complementizer.

00:29:14.385 --> 00:29:15.850 align:middle line:84%
So if your embedded
clause starts

00:29:15.850 --> 00:29:18.280 align:middle line:84%
with "dass," which is the
German word for "that,"

00:29:18.280 --> 00:29:20.830 align:middle line:84%
the German complementizer
for declarative clauses,

00:29:20.830 --> 00:29:22.360 align:middle line:90%
then you don't get V2.

00:29:22.360 --> 00:29:25.120 align:middle line:84%
And main clauses which
don't have a complementizer,

00:29:25.120 --> 00:29:29.320 align:middle line:84%
just like in English,
also have V2.

00:29:29.320 --> 00:29:31.960 align:middle line:84%
So V2 happens whenever there
isn't a complementizer being

00:29:31.960 --> 00:29:33.540 align:middle line:90%
pronounced.

00:29:33.540 --> 00:29:37.690 align:middle line:84%
A hypothesis that people have
had about that goes like this.

00:29:37.690 --> 00:29:40.420 align:middle line:84%
The tree for German
is exactly the tree

00:29:40.420 --> 00:29:41.980 align:middle line:90%
that I drew for you before.

00:29:41.980 --> 00:29:45.280 align:middle line:84%
It's got a complementizer
that precedes its complement.

00:29:45.280 --> 00:29:47.560 align:middle line:84%
And then under that
complementizer,

00:29:47.560 --> 00:29:51.410 align:middle line:84%
everything is head final in
the verbal part of the clause.

00:29:51.410 --> 00:29:54.340 align:middle line:84%
So the auxiliary follows
the verb phrase and the verb

00:29:54.340 --> 00:29:55.510 align:middle line:90%
follows its object.

00:29:55.510 --> 00:29:57.670 align:middle line:84%
And then, yes, German
has prepositions

00:29:57.670 --> 00:30:00.790 align:middle line:84%
ahead of postpositions
because the FOFC resets itself

00:30:00.790 --> 00:30:03.580 align:middle line:84%
across prepositional
phrase boundaries.

00:30:03.580 --> 00:30:07.630 align:middle line:84%
So that's the tree that we
drew before for German clauses.

00:30:07.630 --> 00:30:10.000 align:middle line:84%
Complementizers
go before the TP.

00:30:10.000 --> 00:30:13.000 align:middle line:84%
And then what we're learning is
that if nothing is pronounced

00:30:13.000 --> 00:30:16.300 align:middle line:84%
in the complimentizer-- if
you don't have a "dass"--

00:30:16.300 --> 00:30:18.990 align:middle line:90%
then two things happen.

00:30:18.990 --> 00:30:22.220 align:middle line:84%
First, some phrase-- you get
to pick some phrase at random--

00:30:22.220 --> 00:30:25.370 align:middle line:90%
moves into the specifier of CP.

00:30:25.370 --> 00:30:27.440 align:middle line:84%
So here I've chosen
to move the "sushi"

00:30:27.440 --> 00:30:29.480 align:middle line:90%
into the specifier of CP.

00:30:29.480 --> 00:30:34.450 align:middle line:84%
And also, some
head moves in to C.

00:30:34.450 --> 00:30:36.160 align:middle line:84%
The consequence of
these two movements

00:30:36.160 --> 00:30:38.860 align:middle line:90%
is the pattern that we just saw.

00:30:38.860 --> 00:30:41.680 align:middle line:84%
First of all, the
auxiliary, although we've

00:30:41.680 --> 00:30:43.120 align:middle line:84%
seen that when
there is a "dass,"

00:30:43.120 --> 00:30:44.920 align:middle line:90%
the auxiliary is head final.

00:30:44.920 --> 00:30:47.710 align:middle line:84%
The auxiliary goes where
the complementizer would be.

00:30:47.710 --> 00:30:49.300 align:middle line:90%
It becomes head initial.

00:30:49.300 --> 00:30:52.190 align:middle line:84%
So it goes almost at the
beginning of the sentence,

00:30:52.190 --> 00:30:53.470 align:middle line:90%
but not quite.

00:30:53.470 --> 00:30:56.650 align:middle line:84%
There has to be exactly
one phrase to its left.

00:30:56.650 --> 00:30:58.720 align:middle line:84%
And the story is that's
the phrase that's

00:30:58.720 --> 00:31:02.240 align:middle line:90%
at the left edge of CP.

00:31:02.240 --> 00:31:05.390 align:middle line:84%
Raising many questions,
like "Why, Germans?

00:31:05.390 --> 00:31:06.930 align:middle line:90%
Why are you doing this?"

00:31:06.930 --> 00:31:09.860 align:middle line:84%
But as I say, they do, and there
are many languages out there

00:31:09.860 --> 00:31:10.525 align:middle line:90%
that do this.

00:31:10.525 --> 00:31:11.900 align:middle line:84%
There's a lot of
interesting work

00:31:11.900 --> 00:31:16.160 align:middle line:84%
on how you choose which
phrase to randomly move

00:31:16.160 --> 00:31:17.370 align:middle line:90%
into first position.

00:31:17.370 --> 00:31:19.468 align:middle line:84%
It has consequences
for the interpretation

00:31:19.468 --> 00:31:22.010 align:middle line:84%
of the sentence which are pretty
subtle and difficult to talk

00:31:22.010 --> 00:31:22.510 align:middle line:90%
about.

00:31:22.510 --> 00:31:25.500 align:middle line:84%
There are people who talk about
them, try to figure them out.

00:31:25.500 --> 00:31:28.580 align:middle line:84%
They have to do
with which phrase

00:31:28.580 --> 00:31:31.670 align:middle line:84%
you're trying to draw someone's
attention to, or so on.

00:31:31.670 --> 00:31:35.240 align:middle line:84%
It's very difficult stuff
to talk about seriously.

00:31:35.240 --> 00:31:37.208 align:middle line:84%
But since we're not
doing semantics yet,

00:31:37.208 --> 00:31:38.750 align:middle line:84%
since we're doing
syntax, we can just

00:31:38.750 --> 00:31:40.125 align:middle line:84%
say this is a
thing that happens.

00:31:40.125 --> 00:31:42.140 align:middle line:84%
Some phrase goes
into first position,

00:31:42.140 --> 00:31:47.970 align:middle line:84%
and then whatever's in T
goes into second position.

00:31:47.970 --> 00:31:50.810 align:middle line:84%
So you are moving a phrase
into the specifier of CP

00:31:50.810 --> 00:31:53.210 align:middle line:90%
and you are moving a head--

00:31:53.210 --> 00:31:55.310 align:middle line:84%
in particular, the
head T is moving

00:31:55.310 --> 00:32:00.875 align:middle line:84%
into C. That's how you
get the German V2 order.

00:32:00.875 --> 00:32:01.375 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:32:01.375 --> 00:32:05.220 align:middle line:90%


00:32:05.220 --> 00:32:07.320 align:middle line:90%
You're stunned by German.

00:32:07.320 --> 00:32:08.310 align:middle line:90%
It's understandable.

00:32:08.310 --> 00:32:09.185 align:middle line:90%
It's pretty stunning.

00:32:09.185 --> 00:32:12.090 align:middle line:90%


00:32:12.090 --> 00:32:14.550 align:middle line:84%
Now this is the
part that I thought

00:32:14.550 --> 00:32:17.640 align:middle line:84%
I was going to tell you
before, but now I will.

00:32:17.640 --> 00:32:18.630 align:middle line:90%
It's not just German.

00:32:18.630 --> 00:32:22.590 align:middle line:84%
So there are
languages, some of them

00:32:22.590 --> 00:32:24.600 align:middle line:84%
completely unrelated
to German, others

00:32:24.600 --> 00:32:26.520 align:middle line:84%
only very distantly
related to German,

00:32:26.520 --> 00:32:28.980 align:middle line:90%
that have exactly this setup.

00:32:28.980 --> 00:32:34.770 align:middle line:84%
So Kashmiri is an Indo-Aryan
language spoken in Kashmir

00:32:34.770 --> 00:32:39.730 align:middle line:84%
in which there must be some
phrase in first position.

00:32:39.730 --> 00:32:43.530 align:middle line:84%
And so you say things like
"Ram gave Sham a book."

00:32:43.530 --> 00:32:50.220 align:middle line:84%
Some phrase has to go first,
and when there's no auxiliary,

00:32:50.220 --> 00:32:52.170 align:middle line:84%
the verb goes right
after that first phrase.

00:32:52.170 --> 00:32:54.820 align:middle line:84%
But when there is an auxiliary,
like in the second example,

00:32:54.820 --> 00:32:56.700 align:middle line:84%
the auxiliary goes in
that second position.

00:32:56.700 --> 00:32:59.760 align:middle line:84%
And the verb, in this
case, goes at the end.

00:32:59.760 --> 00:33:01.470 align:middle line:84%
And the thing that
goes in first position

00:33:01.470 --> 00:33:02.850 align:middle line:90%
doesn't have to be the subject.

00:33:02.850 --> 00:33:06.840 align:middle line:84%
You can see that in the last
example, where you've put

00:33:06.840 --> 00:33:08.380 align:middle line:90%
an adverb in first position.

00:33:08.380 --> 00:33:11.862 align:middle line:84%
So Kashmiri is just a very,
very odd dialect of German,

00:33:11.862 --> 00:33:12.570 align:middle line:90%
basically, right?

00:33:12.570 --> 00:33:14.670 align:middle line:84%
So the word order
for-- the rules

00:33:14.670 --> 00:33:16.740 align:middle line:84%
for word order for
Kashmiri resemble

00:33:16.740 --> 00:33:21.120 align:middle line:84%
the rules for word order for
German to a surprising degree.

00:33:21.120 --> 00:33:23.760 align:middle line:84%
I was making a joke when I said
that it's a dialect of German.

00:33:23.760 --> 00:33:24.300 align:middle line:90%
It's not.

00:33:24.300 --> 00:33:26.290 align:middle line:84%
They're both
Indo-European languages,

00:33:26.290 --> 00:33:28.603 align:middle line:84%
so they're related, but
extremely distantly.

00:33:28.603 --> 00:33:30.270 align:middle line:84%
They don't have
anything else in common,

00:33:30.270 --> 00:33:33.540 align:middle line:90%
but they have this in common.

00:33:33.540 --> 00:33:36.600 align:middle line:84%
Vata, which is a Kru
language of Ivory Coast,

00:33:36.600 --> 00:33:40.380 align:middle line:84%
literally not related to
German, also a V2 language.

00:33:40.380 --> 00:33:41.680 align:middle line:90%
And a bunch of others--

00:33:41.680 --> 00:33:44.790 align:middle line:84%
Karitiana, which is a Tupian
language spoken in Brazil.

00:33:44.790 --> 00:33:47.400 align:middle line:84%
Ingush, which is a
Nakh-Daghestanian language

00:33:47.400 --> 00:33:49.560 align:middle line:84%
spoken in the
Caucasus, Dinka, which

00:33:49.560 --> 00:33:52.840 align:middle line:84%
is an Nilontic language spoken
in South Sudan, V2 is a thing.

00:33:52.840 --> 00:33:54.750 align:middle line:84%
It's not a hugely
common thing, but it's

00:33:54.750 --> 00:33:56.730 align:middle line:84%
a thing you find scattered
all over the world.

00:33:56.730 --> 00:33:58.600 align:middle line:84%
There are lots of V2
languages out there.

00:33:58.600 --> 00:34:01.020 align:middle line:84%
That's one of the factory
options for language word

00:34:01.020 --> 00:34:03.390 align:middle line:90%
orders, being V2.

00:34:03.390 --> 00:34:04.680 align:middle line:90%
Lots of languages out there.

00:34:04.680 --> 00:34:08.489 align:middle line:90%


00:34:08.489 --> 00:34:10.550 align:middle line:84%
So V2, the verb second,
is all over the place.

00:34:10.550 --> 00:34:12.800 align:middle line:84%
And in our system,
what it consists of

00:34:12.800 --> 00:34:15.770 align:middle line:84%
is head movement to head
initial C and movement

00:34:15.770 --> 00:34:18.110 align:middle line:84%
of some phrase, some
randomly chosen phrase,

00:34:18.110 --> 00:34:20.260 align:middle line:90%
to the specifier of CP.

00:34:20.260 --> 00:34:24.830 align:middle line:84%
OK. now why am I making
such a big deal about this?

00:34:24.830 --> 00:34:28.070 align:middle line:84%
We know that complementizer
can be final.

00:34:28.070 --> 00:34:31.250 align:middle line:84%
So German complementizers
are head initial.

00:34:31.250 --> 00:34:33.679 align:middle line:84%
We know that by looking at
them when they're overt.

00:34:33.679 --> 00:34:35.719 align:middle line:84%
So German has this
complementizer, "dass," which

00:34:35.719 --> 00:34:36.844 align:middle line:90%
is their version of "that."

00:34:36.844 --> 00:34:40.010 align:middle line:84%
It's actually related to
the English word "that."

00:34:40.010 --> 00:34:41.870 align:middle line:84%
We can see that it
is head initial.

00:34:41.870 --> 00:34:46.040 align:middle line:84%
And we can also see from German
V2 that German V2 involves

00:34:46.040 --> 00:34:49.639 align:middle line:84%
moving the auxiliary-- or
if there is no auxiliary,

00:34:49.639 --> 00:34:50.780 align:middle line:90%
the verb--

00:34:50.780 --> 00:34:54.030 align:middle line:84%
into C, which causes it
to be in second position.

00:34:54.030 --> 00:34:56.270 align:middle line:84%
So now it precedes
the verb phrase,

00:34:56.270 --> 00:34:58.520 align:middle line:84%
precedes the rest of
the clause, and there's

00:34:58.520 --> 00:35:00.440 align:middle line:90%
some phrase in its specifier.

00:35:00.440 --> 00:35:01.940 align:middle line:84%
We know that there
can be languages

00:35:01.940 --> 00:35:02.990 align:middle line:90%
in which C is head final.

00:35:02.990 --> 00:35:04.160 align:middle line:90%
I showed you that earlier.

00:35:04.160 --> 00:35:05.535 align:middle line:84%
There are languages
like Japanese

00:35:05.535 --> 00:35:07.340 align:middle line:90%
in which C goes at the end.

00:35:07.340 --> 00:35:09.043 align:middle line:84%
Japanese has a
word for that too.

00:35:09.043 --> 00:35:09.710 align:middle line:90%
It's [JAPANESE].

00:35:09.710 --> 00:35:13.340 align:middle line:84%
It goes after the clause
that it combines with.

00:35:13.340 --> 00:35:15.950 align:middle line:84%
So it wouldn't be hard to
build the language that

00:35:15.950 --> 00:35:18.560 align:middle line:84%
would be the mirror
image of German, right?

00:35:18.560 --> 00:35:21.380 align:middle line:84%
So this would be a
language in which you'd

00:35:21.380 --> 00:35:24.950 align:middle line:84%
have your clause, your TP,
and it would have in it

00:35:24.950 --> 00:35:28.310 align:middle line:84%
whatever it would have, and
there would be a head final C.

00:35:28.310 --> 00:35:37.178 align:middle line:84%
And you would move some phrase,
XP, into a specifier of CP that

00:35:37.178 --> 00:35:37.970 align:middle line:90%
would be over here.

00:35:37.970 --> 00:35:39.680 align:middle line:90%
This wouldn't be hard, right?

00:35:39.680 --> 00:35:44.663 align:middle line:84%
This is just German, except that
in German, the C is over here

00:35:44.663 --> 00:35:46.580 align:middle line:84%
and the complementizer
that you're moving into

00:35:46.580 --> 00:35:48.450 align:middle line:90%
is over here.

00:35:48.450 --> 00:35:49.640 align:middle line:90%
So this is German.

00:35:49.640 --> 00:35:52.340 align:middle line:84%
There is some phrase
in TP which is

00:35:52.340 --> 00:35:54.740 align:middle line:84%
moving into the specifier
of CP, which is over here.

00:35:54.740 --> 00:35:56.687 align:middle line:90%
C is over here, and this is V2.

00:35:56.687 --> 00:35:57.770 align:middle line:90%
This is the V2 word order.

00:35:57.770 --> 00:36:01.507 align:middle line:84%
This is German and Kashmiri and
Kru and Dinka and, and, and.

00:36:01.507 --> 00:36:03.090 align:middle line:84%
There are lots of
languages like that.

00:36:03.090 --> 00:36:05.940 align:middle line:84%
So are there any
languages like this?

00:36:05.940 --> 00:36:07.560 align:middle line:90%
No.

00:36:07.560 --> 00:36:08.700 align:middle line:90%
None.

00:36:08.700 --> 00:36:11.280 align:middle line:84%
Lots of languages like this,
no languages like that.

00:36:11.280 --> 00:36:14.290 align:middle line:90%
That's not a thing.

00:36:14.290 --> 00:36:16.780 align:middle line:84%
Raising lots of questions
like, well, wait.

00:36:16.780 --> 00:36:18.100 align:middle line:90%
Why is that?

00:36:18.100 --> 00:36:19.450 align:middle line:90%
Because it's not-- right?

00:36:19.450 --> 00:36:22.960 align:middle line:84%
It's not hard to describe
that language on the left.

00:36:22.960 --> 00:36:25.782 align:middle line:84%
But it doesn't-- sorry,
that language on the right.

00:36:25.782 --> 00:36:27.490 align:middle line:84%
The language on the
left is quite common,

00:36:27.490 --> 00:36:29.653 align:middle line:84%
cross linguistically
well attested.

00:36:29.653 --> 00:36:31.195 align:middle line:84%
Language on the
right doesn't happen.

00:36:31.195 --> 00:36:33.920 align:middle line:90%


00:36:33.920 --> 00:36:36.220 align:middle line:84%
So could there be a
language like this?

00:36:36.220 --> 00:36:40.195 align:middle line:84%
No such language
has ever been found.

00:36:40.195 --> 00:36:42.070 align:middle line:84%
And although there are
verb second languages,

00:36:42.070 --> 00:36:44.610 align:middle line:90%
plenty of them--

00:36:44.610 --> 00:36:46.340 align:middle line:84%
and so forget about
trees for a second.

00:36:46.340 --> 00:36:48.380 align:middle line:84%
Suppose you thought--
all of these trees

00:36:48.380 --> 00:36:50.545 align:middle line:84%
I've been showing you,
put them out of your mind.

00:36:50.545 --> 00:36:51.920 align:middle line:84%
We should really
just be thinking

00:36:51.920 --> 00:36:55.080 align:middle line:84%
about verbs and subjects and
objects and things like that.

00:36:55.080 --> 00:36:57.350 align:middle line:90%
We should forget about trees.

00:36:57.350 --> 00:36:59.540 align:middle line:84%
It's a fact that there
are verb second languages.

00:36:59.540 --> 00:37:01.707 align:middle line:84%
Again, plenty of them,
scattered all over the world,

00:37:01.707 --> 00:37:04.100 align:middle line:84%
not related to each other,
not the result of contact.

00:37:04.100 --> 00:37:05.900 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, it's apparently
one of the factory

00:37:05.900 --> 00:37:10.380 align:middle line:84%
options for having a human
language, is to have it V2.

00:37:10.380 --> 00:37:12.150 align:middle line:84%
So you might imagine
that there could also

00:37:12.150 --> 00:37:15.360 align:middle line:84%
be direct object
second languages,

00:37:15.360 --> 00:37:16.590 align:middle line:90%
or subject second language.

00:37:16.590 --> 00:37:19.370 align:middle line:84%
Languages where the subject
had to be the second thing

00:37:19.370 --> 00:37:21.870 align:middle line:84%
and you could put anything you
wanted to before the subject,

00:37:21.870 --> 00:37:22.380 align:middle line:90%
right?

00:37:22.380 --> 00:37:23.940 align:middle line:84%
Or languages where
the direct object

00:37:23.940 --> 00:37:24.960 align:middle line:84%
had to be the second
thing, and you

00:37:24.960 --> 00:37:27.750 align:middle line:84%
could put anything you wanted
to before the direct object.

00:37:27.750 --> 00:37:30.570 align:middle line:84%
I wouldn't know how to draw a
tree for a language like that,

00:37:30.570 --> 00:37:32.970 align:middle line:84%
but it's not any
harder to describe it

00:37:32.970 --> 00:37:35.700 align:middle line:84%
in words than it is to describe
a verb second language.

00:37:35.700 --> 00:37:37.140 align:middle line:90%
Do people see what I mean?

00:37:37.140 --> 00:37:39.090 align:middle line:84%
This is a reason to
take trees seriously

00:37:39.090 --> 00:37:40.650 align:middle line:84%
as a way of
describing languages,

00:37:40.650 --> 00:37:43.320 align:middle line:84%
because they make it
easy to describe German--

00:37:43.320 --> 00:37:46.470 align:middle line:84%
we just did it-- but hard to
describe these other imaginable

00:37:46.470 --> 00:37:48.360 align:middle line:84%
languages, direct
object second languages

00:37:48.360 --> 00:37:49.920 align:middle line:90%
or subject second languages.

00:37:49.920 --> 00:37:52.810 align:middle line:84%
And the fact is that those
languages don't exist.

00:37:52.810 --> 00:37:55.560 align:middle line:84%
So the German kind of
language, the verb second kind

00:37:55.560 --> 00:37:58.802 align:middle line:84%
of language, reasonably
common in the world.

00:37:58.802 --> 00:38:01.260 align:middle line:84%
But these other languages that
are not too hard to imagine,

00:38:01.260 --> 00:38:03.640 align:middle line:90%
they don't happen.

00:38:03.640 --> 00:38:06.930 align:middle line:84%
So we're at another one of these
frustrating points in the class

00:38:06.930 --> 00:38:10.200 align:middle line:90%
where I've shown you a mystery.

00:38:10.200 --> 00:38:11.550 align:middle line:90%
There are verb second languages.

00:38:11.550 --> 00:38:14.160 align:middle line:84%
There are no verb
penultimate languages, mirror

00:38:14.160 --> 00:38:15.152 align:middle line:90%
image of German.

00:38:15.152 --> 00:38:17.610 align:middle line:84%
There are verb second languages,
there are no direct object

00:38:17.610 --> 00:38:19.680 align:middle line:90%
second languages.

00:38:19.680 --> 00:38:22.920 align:middle line:84%
And when I showed you the FOFC,
we were at a similar point.

00:38:22.920 --> 00:38:24.720 align:middle line:84%
The FOFC, it seems
to happen, yeah.

00:38:24.720 --> 00:38:27.900 align:middle line:84%
So there are languages
like English and Japanese

00:38:27.900 --> 00:38:29.190 align:middle line:90%
and German and West Flemish.

00:38:29.190 --> 00:38:31.860 align:middle line:84%
There's another logically
possible kind of language

00:38:31.860 --> 00:38:33.897 align:middle line:84%
where the verb phrase is
head final and the TP--

00:38:33.897 --> 00:38:35.730 align:middle line:84%
sorry, where the verb
phrase is head initial

00:38:35.730 --> 00:38:37.020 align:middle line:90%
and the TP is head final.

00:38:37.020 --> 00:38:38.460 align:middle line:90%
That language doesn't exist.

00:38:38.460 --> 00:38:40.860 align:middle line:84%
And in languages like
Finnish and Basque--

00:38:40.860 --> 00:38:42.540 align:middle line:84%
actually, I didn't
show you Basque--

00:38:42.540 --> 00:38:45.930 align:middle line:84%
where word order is fairly free
under certain circumstances,

00:38:45.930 --> 00:38:48.600 align:middle line:84%
that particular word
order is ruled out.

00:38:48.600 --> 00:38:52.050 align:middle line:84%
And then I said, sure
would be nice to know why.

00:38:52.050 --> 00:38:55.295 align:middle line:84%
And if this were an
intro to syntax class,

00:38:55.295 --> 00:38:57.420 align:middle line:84%
this is where I would start
trying to show you why,

00:38:57.420 --> 00:38:59.850 align:middle line:84%
or showing you people's
theories of why.

00:38:59.850 --> 00:39:03.355 align:middle line:84%
But instead, this is 24.900, and
we've already spent many days

00:39:03.355 --> 00:39:05.730 align:middle line:84%
talking about syntax, and I
really have to start teaching

00:39:05.730 --> 00:39:06.600 align:middle line:90%
you other things soon.

00:39:06.600 --> 00:39:08.100 align:middle line:84%
So I will just tell
you, if you would

00:39:08.100 --> 00:39:09.933 align:middle line:84%
like to know the answers
to these questions,

00:39:09.933 --> 00:39:11.308 align:middle line:84%
go take more
linguistics classes.

00:39:11.308 --> 00:39:12.766 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, these are
the kinds of things

00:39:12.766 --> 00:39:14.790 align:middle line:84%
linguists talk about
and try to figure out.

00:39:14.790 --> 00:39:20.470 align:middle line:90%


00:39:20.470 --> 00:39:23.780 align:middle line:90%
So, moral of all this--

00:39:23.780 --> 00:39:26.210 align:middle line:84%
there are different kinds
of languages in the world,

00:39:26.210 --> 00:39:29.600 align:middle line:84%
but the languages that
we find in the world

00:39:29.600 --> 00:39:32.540 align:middle line:84%
differ in ways that
are constrained.

00:39:32.540 --> 00:39:35.900 align:middle line:84%
So we don't find every
imaginable kind of language.

00:39:35.900 --> 00:39:37.880 align:middle line:84%
There are gaps-- sort
of interesting gaps.

00:39:37.880 --> 00:39:40.820 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes, gaps
that you can define

00:39:40.820 --> 00:39:44.450 align:middle line:84%
the boundaries of, like with
the FOFC, where you say, yeah,

00:39:44.450 --> 00:39:47.060 align:middle line:84%
languages get to decide
for particular heads

00:39:47.060 --> 00:39:48.980 align:middle line:84%
whether they proceed or
follow their sisters,

00:39:48.980 --> 00:39:51.940 align:middle line:84%
but there are certain
patterns that you don't find,

00:39:51.940 --> 00:39:55.166 align:middle line:84%
and we want to have
a theory of why.

00:39:55.166 --> 00:39:57.050 align:middle line:84%
Wh-movements--
there are languages

00:39:57.050 --> 00:39:58.100 align:middle line:90%
that have wh-movement.

00:39:58.100 --> 00:39:59.520 align:middle line:84%
There are languages
that lack it.

00:39:59.520 --> 00:40:01.490 align:middle line:84%
But if you have it,
it goes to the left.

00:40:01.490 --> 00:40:02.960 align:middle line:90%
It never goes to the right.

00:40:02.960 --> 00:40:05.090 align:middle line:84%
There are languages
that have V2.

00:40:05.090 --> 00:40:07.040 align:middle line:84%
There are languages
that don't have V2.

00:40:07.040 --> 00:40:10.535 align:middle line:84%
But there aren't
languages that have V next

00:40:10.535 --> 00:40:14.030 align:middle line:90%
to last, a mirror image of V2.

00:40:14.030 --> 00:40:16.640 align:middle line:84%
There is variation
among languages,

00:40:16.640 --> 00:40:19.250 align:middle line:84%
but there are kinds of things
that you don't ever find,

00:40:19.250 --> 00:40:22.472 align:middle line:84%
certain peculiar patterns that
resolutely fail to show up.

00:40:22.472 --> 00:40:24.680 align:middle line:84%
This is the kind of thing
we're talking about when we

00:40:24.680 --> 00:40:26.240 align:middle line:90%
talk about universal grammar.

00:40:26.240 --> 00:40:28.550 align:middle line:84%
So that's a phrase you may
hear people talk about.

00:40:28.550 --> 00:40:31.080 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes you hear it
used as a term of abuse.

00:40:31.080 --> 00:40:33.373 align:middle line:84%
So there are people who
think that it's a dumb idea,

00:40:33.373 --> 00:40:34.790 align:middle line:84%
you know, that
it's something that

00:40:34.790 --> 00:40:37.782 align:middle line:84%
linguists care about for some
reason, but nobody else should.

00:40:37.782 --> 00:40:39.740 align:middle line:84%
But this is what we mean
when we talk about it.

00:40:39.740 --> 00:40:43.340 align:middle line:84%
We mean it looks as though
part of being a human being

00:40:43.340 --> 00:40:45.920 align:middle line:84%
is having the kind of mind
that can build language

00:40:45.920 --> 00:40:47.880 align:middle line:90%
in some ways, but not others.

00:40:47.880 --> 00:40:49.810 align:middle line:84%
And that's what
universal grammar is.

00:40:49.810 --> 00:40:51.560 align:middle line:84%
It's kind of a bad
name for it, because it

00:40:51.560 --> 00:40:57.020 align:middle line:84%
sounds like we all start
out with the grammar of, I

00:40:57.020 --> 00:40:58.370 align:middle line:90%
don't know, Basque, you know?

00:40:58.370 --> 00:41:00.140 align:middle line:84%
And then we learn
our native languages

00:41:00.140 --> 00:41:02.780 align:middle line:84%
by manipulating the universal
grammar that we all start with.

00:41:02.780 --> 00:41:03.860 align:middle line:90%
That's not the idea.

00:41:03.860 --> 00:41:06.470 align:middle line:84%
The idea is, we're
constrained in the kinds

00:41:06.470 --> 00:41:09.180 align:middle line:84%
of linguistic hypotheses
that we can have.

00:41:09.180 --> 00:41:13.280 align:middle line:84%
And so we come preloaded with
some instructions about how

00:41:13.280 --> 00:41:17.000 align:middle line:84%
to build language, and
there are certain kinds

00:41:17.000 --> 00:41:19.845 align:middle line:84%
of options that we know
better than to even consider

00:41:19.845 --> 00:41:21.470 align:middle line:84%
when we're learning
our first language.

00:41:21.470 --> 00:41:26.090 align:middle line:84%
That's what universal
grammar is meant to claim.

00:41:26.090 --> 00:41:27.666 align:middle line:90%
Questions about any of that?

00:41:27.666 --> 00:41:29.860 align:middle line:90%
Does any of this make sense?

00:41:29.860 --> 00:41:32.990 align:middle line:84%
This is meant to
inoculate you against--

00:41:32.990 --> 00:41:36.760 align:middle line:84%
so in popular science,
you will sometimes

00:41:36.760 --> 00:41:38.980 align:middle line:84%
read people claiming that
universal grammar can't

00:41:38.980 --> 00:41:41.590 align:middle line:84%
possibly be true or it's
dead or it's been disproven

00:41:41.590 --> 00:41:42.230 align:middle line:90%
or whatever.

00:41:42.230 --> 00:41:44.230 align:middle line:90%
And this is all hooey.

00:41:44.230 --> 00:41:48.260 align:middle line:84%
You should throw away any
papers that seem to claim that.

00:41:48.260 --> 00:41:50.915 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:41:50.915 --> 00:41:53.110 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:41:53.110 --> 00:41:55.570 align:middle line:84%
So, yeah, in this particular
case, no V-penultimate,

00:41:55.570 --> 00:41:58.000 align:middle line:90%
no wh-movement to the right.

00:41:58.000 --> 00:42:00.833 align:middle line:84%
It's as though maybe heads
can either proceed or follow

00:42:00.833 --> 00:42:02.500 align:middle line:84%
their sisters with
certain restrictions,

00:42:02.500 --> 00:42:05.380 align:middle line:84%
as we've seen in the FOFC,
but specifiers maybe always

00:42:05.380 --> 00:42:06.430 align:middle line:90%
proceed their sisters.

00:42:06.430 --> 00:42:09.850 align:middle line:84%
And again, we'd
want to know why.

00:42:09.850 --> 00:42:11.560 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:42:11.560 --> 00:42:16.270 align:middle line:84%
So we've now-- so
here I am pivoting.

00:42:16.270 --> 00:42:20.150 align:middle line:84%
You can tell because the
font size is changing.

00:42:20.150 --> 00:42:23.780 align:middle line:84%
We've now seen several different
kinds of movement operations.

00:42:23.780 --> 00:42:25.990 align:middle line:84%
So we've talked about
the wh-movement,

00:42:25.990 --> 00:42:28.240 align:middle line:84%
which is what makes questions
like "What did you buy?"

00:42:28.240 --> 00:42:31.090 align:middle line:84%
where "what"-- wh moves
to the specifier of CP,

00:42:31.090 --> 00:42:33.940 align:middle line:84%
which is on the left
periphery of the clause.

00:42:33.940 --> 00:42:36.250 align:middle line:84%
We've now talked
about head movement.

00:42:36.250 --> 00:42:38.470 align:middle line:84%
That was part of
talking about German V2.

00:42:38.470 --> 00:42:42.820 align:middle line:84%
So in V2, which handily
I still have a tree for,

00:42:42.820 --> 00:42:45.130 align:middle line:84%
some phrase moves to
the specifier of CP,

00:42:45.130 --> 00:42:48.940 align:middle line:84%
and T moves into
C. And that's why

00:42:48.940 --> 00:42:52.840 align:middle line:84%
you get word orders like
a prepositional phrase,

00:42:52.840 --> 00:43:01.110 align:middle line:84%
"With chopsticks
will she sushi eat,"

00:43:01.110 --> 00:43:03.510 align:middle line:84%
which is how you would
say that in German.

00:43:03.510 --> 00:43:05.730 align:middle line:84%
So you'd have some
phrase in first position,

00:43:05.730 --> 00:43:08.720 align:middle line:84%
and then whatever it
is that bears tense--

00:43:08.720 --> 00:43:10.440 align:middle line:84%
so the highest
auxiliary, if there's

00:43:10.440 --> 00:43:13.410 align:middle line:84%
an auxiliary, or the verb, if
there's no auxiliary-- ends up

00:43:13.410 --> 00:43:16.200 align:middle line:84%
in C. And I just casually
said, "Hey, look,

00:43:16.200 --> 00:43:17.910 align:middle line:84%
this head is moving
to this other head."

00:43:17.910 --> 00:43:20.577 align:middle line:90%
That's called head movement.

00:43:20.577 --> 00:43:22.410 align:middle line:84%
I do a lot of head
movement as I'm teaching.

00:43:22.410 --> 00:43:24.960 align:middle line:90%
Some of you may have noticed.

00:43:24.960 --> 00:43:27.330 align:middle line:84%
And then we've also
talked about NP-movement.

00:43:27.330 --> 00:43:29.460 align:middle line:84%
That was the case where
you took a noun phrase

00:43:29.460 --> 00:43:31.740 align:middle line:84%
and moved it into
the specifier of TP.

00:43:31.740 --> 00:43:34.980 align:middle line:90%
So three kinds of movement then.

00:43:34.980 --> 00:43:39.375 align:middle line:90%
We've got "What did you buy?"

00:43:39.375 --> 00:43:42.240 align:middle line:90%


00:43:42.240 --> 00:43:45.510 align:middle line:90%
where "what" is wh-moving.

00:43:45.510 --> 00:43:48.460 align:middle line:90%


00:43:48.460 --> 00:43:51.810 align:middle line:90%
We've got German.

00:43:51.810 --> 00:44:01.710 align:middle line:84%
So in German, "With
chopsticks will

00:44:01.710 --> 00:44:05.570 align:middle line:84%
she sushi eat,"
where we have reasons

00:44:05.570 --> 00:44:07.320 align:middle line:84%
to think that auxiliaries
start at the end

00:44:07.320 --> 00:44:08.910 align:middle line:90%
of the cause and head move.

00:44:08.910 --> 00:44:12.090 align:middle line:90%
So here's head movement.

00:44:12.090 --> 00:44:14.430 align:middle line:84%
And then the last
one is NP-movement,

00:44:14.430 --> 00:44:17.310 align:middle line:84%
and that was how we were
going to deal with sentences

00:44:17.310 --> 00:44:24.840 align:middle line:84%
like "The sushi was
devoured," where

00:44:24.840 --> 00:44:27.510 align:middle line:84%
we think that "devour"
selects for an object,

00:44:27.510 --> 00:44:28.770 align:middle line:90%
needs to have an object.

00:44:28.770 --> 00:44:30.690 align:middle line:84%
But in this case, its
object isn't there

00:44:30.690 --> 00:44:31.980 align:middle line:90%
because it's moved over here.

00:44:31.980 --> 00:44:33.960 align:middle line:90%
This is NP-movement.

00:44:33.960 --> 00:44:35.040 align:middle line:90%
It's moved over here.

00:44:35.040 --> 00:44:39.120 align:middle line:84%
Because of the EPP, TP
needs to have a specifier,

00:44:39.120 --> 00:44:41.970 align:middle line:84%
and the sushi has raised to
become the specifier of TP.

00:44:41.970 --> 00:44:45.570 align:middle line:84%
So wh-movement movement and head
movement and NP-movement, and I

00:44:45.570 --> 00:44:47.730 align:middle line:84%
can see that I've used my
own private abbreviation

00:44:47.730 --> 00:44:50.490 align:middle line:90%
for movements, which is mvmt.

00:44:50.490 --> 00:44:52.107 align:middle line:84%
Because if you're
a syntactician,

00:44:52.107 --> 00:44:53.940 align:middle line:84%
you have to write the
word "movement" a lot.

00:44:53.940 --> 00:44:56.850 align:middle line:84%
So that's my abbreviation
for movement.

00:44:56.850 --> 00:44:59.517 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so three kinds of movement
that we've talked about so far.

00:44:59.517 --> 00:45:01.267 align:middle line:84%
Are there any questions
about any of that?

00:45:01.267 --> 00:45:03.690 align:middle line:84%
Because we're about to
talk about those movements

00:45:03.690 --> 00:45:05.670 align:middle line:84%
and their properties in
a little bit of detail.

00:45:05.670 --> 00:45:08.467 align:middle line:90%


00:45:08.467 --> 00:45:08.967 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:45:08.967 --> 00:45:12.270 align:middle line:90%


00:45:12.270 --> 00:45:15.420 align:middle line:90%
So "Mary will type novels."

00:45:15.420 --> 00:45:17.693 align:middle line:84%
We've talked about
head movements.

00:45:17.693 --> 00:45:19.360 align:middle line:84%
We haven't talked
about it in this case,

00:45:19.360 --> 00:45:21.360 align:middle line:84%
but here's a place where
we could talk about it.

00:45:21.360 --> 00:45:24.520 align:middle line:84%
There's a way to ask questions,
yes/no questions, where

00:45:24.520 --> 00:45:27.100 align:middle line:84%
you take whatever's in T
and you move it into C.

00:45:27.100 --> 00:45:29.200 align:middle line:84%
So you start with "Mary
will type novels."

00:45:29.200 --> 00:45:32.830 align:middle line:84%
You move T into C, and so you
have the word order "Will Mary

00:45:32.830 --> 00:45:33.520 align:middle line:90%
type novels?"

00:45:33.520 --> 00:45:36.280 align:middle line:84%
So the auxiliary now
precedes the subject.

00:45:36.280 --> 00:45:38.710 align:middle line:84%
So here's an instance of
head movement-- in this case,

00:45:38.710 --> 00:45:40.720 align:middle line:84%
in order to form yes/no
questions in English.

00:45:40.720 --> 00:45:43.540 align:middle line:90%


00:45:43.540 --> 00:45:46.850 align:middle line:84%
Wh-questions, "What
what will Mary type?"

00:45:46.850 --> 00:45:49.250 align:middle line:84%
Not only are you
moving T into C--

00:45:49.250 --> 00:45:51.540 align:middle line:84%
so "will" is moving from
the position after "Mary"

00:45:51.540 --> 00:45:53.540 align:middle line:84%
to the position before
"Mary"-- but you're doing

00:45:53.540 --> 00:45:56.060 align:middle line:84%
the wh-movement that
we've talked about before.

00:45:56.060 --> 00:45:59.840 align:middle line:84%
The object is moving
into the specifier of CP.

00:45:59.840 --> 00:46:03.493 align:middle line:84%
And then in NP-movement,
"The cookies were devoured."

00:46:03.493 --> 00:46:04.910 align:middle line:84%
We've taken the
object of "devour"

00:46:04.910 --> 00:46:08.400 align:middle line:84%
and moved it in the spec of TP,
because TP needs to specify.

00:46:08.400 --> 00:46:08.900 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:46:08.900 --> 00:46:11.600 align:middle line:90%


00:46:11.600 --> 00:46:13.280 align:middle line:84%
We've seen that not
all languages have

00:46:13.280 --> 00:46:14.750 align:middle line:90%
all of these kinds of movement.

00:46:14.750 --> 00:46:17.420 align:middle line:84%
So there are languages
with wh-in-situ.

00:46:17.420 --> 00:46:19.625 align:middle line:90%
This is the Chaha example.

00:46:19.625 --> 00:46:21.630 align:middle line:84%
Chaha is a language
spoken in Ethiopia.

00:46:21.630 --> 00:46:23.480 align:middle line:90%
It's a Semitic language.

00:46:23.480 --> 00:46:28.310 align:middle line:84%
It was actually-- I teach
the graduate field methods

00:46:28.310 --> 00:46:36.067 align:middle line:84%
class and Chaha was the first
language that we worked on.

00:46:36.067 --> 00:46:38.150 align:middle line:84%
That's the one-- I think
I've told you this story.

00:46:38.150 --> 00:46:41.600 align:middle line:84%
That's the one in which I tried
to start the class by saying

00:46:41.600 --> 00:46:45.500 align:middle line:84%
"The man cooked the meat," and
the guy who we were working

00:46:45.500 --> 00:46:47.100 align:middle line:84%
with said, "No, I
cannot say that."

00:46:47.100 --> 00:46:47.880 align:middle line:90%
And I said, "Why?"

00:46:47.880 --> 00:46:49.370 align:middle line:90%
He said, "Men do not cook."

00:46:49.370 --> 00:46:51.170 align:middle line:90%
It was like, oh, OK.

00:46:51.170 --> 00:46:55.470 align:middle line:84%
So, Chaha-- he also taught us an
expression for "very quickly,"

00:46:55.470 --> 00:46:58.883 align:middle line:84%
which meant "in the time it
takes spit to evaporate,"

00:46:58.883 --> 00:47:00.800 align:middle line:84%
which I thought was a
cool expression to have.

00:47:00.800 --> 00:47:02.842 align:middle line:84%
I wish I could remember
how to say that in Chaha.

00:47:02.842 --> 00:47:04.560 align:middle line:90%
I should go look it up.

00:47:04.560 --> 00:47:06.440 align:middle line:84%
So Chahah is a
wh-in-situ language.

00:47:06.440 --> 00:47:08.970 align:middle line:84%
It leaves the wh-phrase
where objects normally go.

00:47:08.970 --> 00:47:10.340 align:middle line:90%
It's also a head final language.

00:47:10.340 --> 00:47:14.660 align:middle line:84%
It has the same word
order as Japanese.

00:47:14.660 --> 00:47:16.170 align:middle line:90%
It's a wh-in-situ language.

00:47:16.170 --> 00:47:18.140 align:middle line:84%
So there are languages
that leave wh in-situ.

00:47:18.140 --> 00:47:19.580 align:middle line:90%
They don't move it.

00:47:19.580 --> 00:47:22.130 align:middle line:84%
There are languages that
don't go in for NP movement,

00:47:22.130 --> 00:47:23.630 align:middle line:84%
at least as much
as English does.

00:47:23.630 --> 00:47:27.440 align:middle line:84%
So in English, I
would have to say,

00:47:27.440 --> 00:47:29.090 align:middle line:90%
"The cookies have been eaten."

00:47:29.090 --> 00:47:31.760 align:middle line:84%
But in Italian, you can say
something that literally means

00:47:31.760 --> 00:47:33.200 align:middle line:90%
"Have been eaten the cookies."

00:47:33.200 --> 00:47:35.480 align:middle line:84%
So the cookies can just
stay in object position.

00:47:35.480 --> 00:47:37.550 align:middle line:90%
The Italians don't have the EPP.

00:47:37.550 --> 00:47:40.460 align:middle line:84%
They're very relaxed about
whether TP has a specifier

00:47:40.460 --> 00:47:42.260 align:middle line:84%
or not, so the cookies
can just stay where.

00:47:42.260 --> 00:47:44.400 align:middle line:84%
They are they don't
have to go anywhere.

00:47:44.400 --> 00:47:46.550 align:middle line:84%
So Chaha doesn't have
to move its wh-phrases.

00:47:46.550 --> 00:47:48.717 align:middle line:84%
Italian doesn't have
to move its NPs.

00:47:48.717 --> 00:47:51.050 align:middle line:84%
There are also cross-linguistic
differences with respect

00:47:51.050 --> 00:47:52.980 align:middle line:90%
to where heads go.

00:47:52.980 --> 00:47:58.340 align:middle line:84%
So here's an English sentence,
"Mary often speaks French,"

00:47:58.340 --> 00:48:00.680 align:middle line:90%
where I've put an adverb.

00:48:00.680 --> 00:48:03.050 align:middle line:90%
I've adjoined-- it's an adjunct.

00:48:03.050 --> 00:48:06.950 align:middle line:84%
It's not selected by anything,
that adverb phrase, "often."

00:48:06.950 --> 00:48:09.240 align:middle line:84%
I've attached it to
the verb phrase there.

00:48:09.240 --> 00:48:12.920 align:middle line:84%
And then you've got a verb with
its object, "speaks French."

00:48:12.920 --> 00:48:16.880 align:middle line:84%
If any of you speak French--
does anybody speak French?

00:48:16.880 --> 00:48:19.430 align:middle line:84%
So if any of you know any
French-- a couple of you do,

00:48:19.430 --> 00:48:20.090 align:middle line:90%
it looks like.

00:48:20.090 --> 00:48:20.998 align:middle line:90%
Maybe you know.

00:48:20.998 --> 00:48:22.790 align:middle line:84%
This isn't the right
word order for French.

00:48:22.790 --> 00:48:25.430 align:middle line:84%
The right word order
for French involves

00:48:25.430 --> 00:48:28.790 align:middle line:84%
taking the word for "speaks"
and putting it before "often."

00:48:28.790 --> 00:48:32.780 align:middle line:84%
You can't, in French, say
"Mary often speaks French."

00:48:32.780 --> 00:48:36.830 align:middle line:84%
You must literally say,
"Mary speaks often French,"

00:48:36.830 --> 00:48:39.050 align:middle line:90%
which in English, we cannot say.

00:48:39.050 --> 00:48:43.850 align:middle line:84%
So this is another basic
difference between languages.

00:48:43.850 --> 00:48:46.790 align:middle line:84%
French requires the verb
to raise to T. Sorry,

00:48:46.790 --> 00:48:47.720 align:middle line:90%
I called it I again.

00:48:47.720 --> 00:48:49.850 align:middle line:90%
I'll try to fix that.

00:48:49.850 --> 00:48:51.500 align:middle line:84%
French requires
the verb to raise

00:48:51.500 --> 00:48:54.140 align:middle line:84%
to T when there's
no auxiliary, so you

00:48:54.140 --> 00:48:57.200 align:middle line:90%
say "Mary speaks often French."

00:48:57.200 --> 00:48:59.690 align:middle line:84%
English doesn't require
the verb to raise to T.

00:48:59.690 --> 00:49:04.880 align:middle line:84%
So just like English requires
the wh-phrase to move

00:49:04.880 --> 00:49:07.190 align:middle line:84%
to the specifier of
CP and Chaha doesn't--

00:49:07.190 --> 00:49:09.950 align:middle line:84%
Mandarin doesn't,
Japanese doesn't--

00:49:09.950 --> 00:49:14.600 align:middle line:84%
French requires the verb to
raise to T, to move to T.

00:49:14.600 --> 00:49:15.432 align:middle line:90%
English doesn't.

00:49:15.432 --> 00:49:17.390 align:middle line:84%
Yes, so there are
differences between languages

00:49:17.390 --> 00:49:20.060 align:middle line:84%
along these lines, where
one language will have

00:49:20.060 --> 00:49:21.780 align:middle line:84%
one set of movements
and another language

00:49:21.780 --> 00:49:23.238 align:middle line:84%
will have another
set of movements.

00:49:23.238 --> 00:49:24.500 align:middle line:90%
That's a thing we find.

00:49:24.500 --> 00:49:27.060 align:middle line:84%
It would be nice to know
why and try to derive this

00:49:27.060 --> 00:49:31.020 align:middle line:84%
from something else, but that's
where the field is right now.

00:49:31.020 --> 00:49:33.480 align:middle line:84%
I haven't talked
very much about why

00:49:33.480 --> 00:49:36.847 align:middle line:84%
these movements happen,
and I won't, mainly

00:49:36.847 --> 00:49:37.680 align:middle line:90%
for reasons of time.

00:49:37.680 --> 00:49:38.860 align:middle line:90%
It's a very interesting topic.

00:49:38.860 --> 00:49:39.870 align:middle line:84%
There's a lot of
work on it in trying

00:49:39.870 --> 00:49:42.078 align:middle line:84%
to figure out what it is
that drives these movements,

00:49:42.078 --> 00:49:43.210 align:middle line:90%
causes them to happen.

00:49:43.210 --> 00:49:44.960 align:middle line:84%
So the only one that
I've really given you

00:49:44.960 --> 00:49:48.630 align:middle line:84%
a motivation for is
NP-movement, which I've said

00:49:48.630 --> 00:49:51.572 align:middle line:84%
is driven by the
EPP, the need for TP

00:49:51.572 --> 00:49:53.280 align:middle line:84%
to have a specifier,
which is a need that

00:49:53.280 --> 00:49:55.280 align:middle line:84%
exists in some languages,
but not others.

00:49:55.280 --> 00:49:59.040 align:middle line:84%
So English has that, and
Italian, for example, doesn't.

00:49:59.040 --> 00:50:01.620 align:middle line:84%
But I do want to talk some--
so I haven't talked about why

00:50:01.620 --> 00:50:02.310 align:middle line:90%
these movements happen.

00:50:02.310 --> 00:50:04.477 align:middle line:84%
But I want to talk some
about some of the conditions

00:50:04.477 --> 00:50:05.040 align:middle line:90%
on movement.

00:50:05.040 --> 00:50:07.110 align:middle line:84%
Because what we're going to see
is that movement can't always

00:50:07.110 --> 00:50:09.585 align:middle line:84%
happen, and we're going to
want to try to understand why.

00:50:09.585 --> 00:50:13.970 align:middle line:90%


00:50:13.970 --> 00:50:16.870 align:middle line:84%
So you can say things
like "I ordered

00:50:16.870 --> 00:50:21.180 align:middle line:84%
a hamburger and French
fries," but it would be weird

00:50:21.180 --> 00:50:23.400 align:middle line:84%
for me to ask you a
question like, "What did you

00:50:23.400 --> 00:50:25.880 align:middle line:90%
order a hamburger and?"

00:50:25.880 --> 00:50:27.140 align:middle line:90%
Do people agree?

00:50:27.140 --> 00:50:29.418 align:middle line:84%
That's a strange
question to ask.

00:50:29.418 --> 00:50:31.460 align:middle line:84%
So it's sometimes called
the coordinate structure

00:50:31.460 --> 00:50:33.000 align:middle line:90%
constraint.

00:50:33.000 --> 00:50:35.150 align:middle line:84%
And it's one of
many examples where

00:50:35.150 --> 00:50:41.390 align:middle line:84%
people have found the
wh-movement example is somehow

00:50:41.390 --> 00:50:42.590 align:middle line:90%
blocked.

00:50:42.590 --> 00:50:45.740 align:middle line:84%
So all of the wh-movement
examples I've shown you so far,

00:50:45.740 --> 00:50:48.410 align:middle line:84%
it's always been possible
to move the wh-phrase.

00:50:48.410 --> 00:50:50.100 align:middle line:84%
There are times when
it's not possible,

00:50:50.100 --> 00:50:51.270 align:middle line:90%
and this is one example.

00:50:51.270 --> 00:50:53.720 align:middle line:84%
So "What did you order
a hamburger and?"

00:50:53.720 --> 00:50:54.350 align:middle line:90%
No good.

00:50:54.350 --> 00:50:57.470 align:middle line:84%
It's roughly if you
have two things that

00:50:57.470 --> 00:51:00.650 align:middle line:84%
are connected by "and," you
can't wh-move one of them.

00:51:00.650 --> 00:51:04.407 align:middle line:84%
You also can't say "What did
you order and a hamburger,"

00:51:04.407 --> 00:51:06.740 align:middle line:84%
where you moved the first one
instead of the second one.

00:51:06.740 --> 00:51:07.670 align:middle line:90%
Also no good.

00:51:07.670 --> 00:51:11.200 align:middle line:90%


00:51:11.200 --> 00:51:13.030 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:51:13.030 --> 00:51:14.540 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:51:14.540 --> 00:51:16.540 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: The second one
sounded fine, actually.

00:51:16.540 --> 00:51:18.248 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: I'm
sorry, say it again?

00:51:18.248 --> 00:51:19.992 align:middle line:90%
The second one sounded fine?

00:51:19.992 --> 00:51:20.950 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: I don't know.

00:51:20.950 --> 00:51:22.742 align:middle line:84%
Like, "What did you
order a hamburger and?"

00:51:22.742 --> 00:51:25.360 align:middle line:90%


00:51:25.360 --> 00:51:27.580 align:middle line:84%
Like, I have to
somehow think, oh,

00:51:27.580 --> 00:51:29.543 align:middle line:84%
that is not
grammatically correct.

00:51:29.543 --> 00:51:30.460 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah?

00:51:30.460 --> 00:51:34.462 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: It's just on--
like, if someone just asked me

00:51:34.462 --> 00:51:36.877 align:middle line:90%
that, I would just [INAUDIBLE].

00:51:36.877 --> 00:51:41.230 align:middle line:90%


00:51:41.230 --> 00:51:43.060 align:middle line:90%
It's somehow-- no, the first on.

00:51:43.060 --> 00:51:43.420 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: The first one.

00:51:43.420 --> 00:51:44.080 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, this one.

00:51:44.080 --> 00:51:45.430 align:middle line:84%
"What did you order
a hamburger and?"

00:51:45.430 --> 00:51:47.388 align:middle line:84%
You mean if somebody
asked you that, you'd just

00:51:47.388 --> 00:51:48.537 align:middle line:90%
be like, "French fries."

00:51:48.537 --> 00:51:49.120 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Huh?

00:51:49.120 --> 00:51:51.078 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: You'd
just say "French fries."

00:51:51.078 --> 00:51:52.560 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:51:52.560 --> 00:51:54.720 align:middle line:84%
Like, now that I'm
listening to it,

00:51:54.720 --> 00:51:56.170 align:middle line:90%
it's grammatically incorrect.

00:51:56.170 --> 00:51:57.420 align:middle line:90%
Now that I'm playing it back.

00:51:57.420 --> 00:51:57.870 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:51:57.870 --> 00:52:00.105 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: But it just
somehow doesn't sound weird.

00:52:00.105 --> 00:52:00.600 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Hm.

00:52:00.600 --> 00:52:01.600 align:middle line:90%
Oh, that's interesting.

00:52:01.600 --> 00:52:04.265 align:middle line:84%
I mean, several other people
are raising their hand,

00:52:04.265 --> 00:52:06.390 align:middle line:84%
so maybe I'll just open
this to general discussion.

00:52:06.390 --> 00:52:07.925 align:middle line:90%
Joseph?

00:52:07.925 --> 00:52:09.300 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I feel
like it would be

00:52:09.300 --> 00:52:11.112 align:middle line:84%
natural to put a
colon after "order."

00:52:11.112 --> 00:52:13.320 align:middle line:84%
I don't know what that would
do, like, syntactically,

00:52:13.320 --> 00:52:14.970 align:middle line:90%
but "What did you order--

00:52:14.970 --> 00:52:17.710 align:middle line:90%
a hamburger and?"

00:52:17.710 --> 00:52:20.920 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah, so
forget about "and" for a second.

00:52:20.920 --> 00:52:25.220 align:middle line:84%
I think it's possible to
say, OK, "you ordered--"

00:52:25.220 --> 00:52:26.960 align:middle line:84%
and have that be
a question, right?

00:52:26.960 --> 00:52:29.520 align:middle line:84%
So it's your job to fill
in the blank for me.

00:52:29.520 --> 00:52:32.843 align:middle line:84%
And I think what you just did
is a version of that where

00:52:32.843 --> 00:52:34.010 align:middle line:90%
I say, "What did you order?"

00:52:34.010 --> 00:52:35.330 align:middle line:90%
That's the wh-question.

00:52:35.330 --> 00:52:37.160 align:middle line:84%
And now I'm going
to ask you to help

00:52:37.160 --> 00:52:38.450 align:middle line:90%
me finish the sentence for me.

00:52:38.450 --> 00:52:39.950 align:middle line:90%
I think that's what's going on.

00:52:39.950 --> 00:52:41.090 align:middle line:90%
Kateryna?

00:52:41.090 --> 00:52:44.210 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: When you say
you ordered dot, dot, dot,

00:52:44.210 --> 00:52:50.700 align:middle line:84%
is that equivalent to not
doing wh-movement and just not

00:52:50.700 --> 00:52:52.840 align:middle line:90%
pronouncing the "what?"

00:52:52.840 --> 00:52:56.240 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: It's
an interesting question.

00:52:56.240 --> 00:52:59.080 align:middle line:84%
I don't know whether we want
the syntax to handle that

00:52:59.080 --> 00:53:04.280 align:middle line:84%
or not, or whether we want that
to be like the kind of case

00:53:04.280 --> 00:53:05.270 align:middle line:90%
I talked about before.

00:53:05.270 --> 00:53:09.070 align:middle line:90%


00:53:09.070 --> 00:53:12.410 align:middle line:90%
So I-- because I think--

00:53:12.410 --> 00:53:16.040 align:middle line:84%
there's a restriction
on that technique.

00:53:16.040 --> 00:53:18.800 align:middle line:84%
For example, it has to be
the end of the sentence.

00:53:18.800 --> 00:53:21.080 align:middle line:84%
You have to stop talking
before you get to the end.

00:53:21.080 --> 00:53:26.120 align:middle line:84%
I can't say "you ordered
and a hamburger,"

00:53:26.120 --> 00:53:28.940 align:middle line:90%
I don't think, right?

00:53:28.940 --> 00:53:33.890 align:middle line:84%
So I think what I'm
doing is simulating

00:53:33.890 --> 00:53:37.250 align:middle line:84%
forgetting how my sentence
was going to end, right?

00:53:37.250 --> 00:53:41.840 align:middle line:84%
So it's really as though I'm
about to say "You ordered

00:53:41.840 --> 00:53:44.360 align:middle line:84%
a hamburger" and I'm
pretending that I can't

00:53:44.360 --> 00:53:45.830 align:middle line:84%
remember how to
end the sentence,

00:53:45.830 --> 00:53:48.780 align:middle line:90%
and I'm asking you to help me.

00:53:48.780 --> 00:53:50.510 align:middle line:84%
I have a feeling
that's the kind of way

00:53:50.510 --> 00:53:54.960 align:middle line:84%
we want to think about that
kind of example, but yeah.

00:53:54.960 --> 00:53:56.790 align:middle line:90%
Raequel?

00:53:56.790 --> 00:53:58.767 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: When I hear,
"What did you order?"

00:53:58.767 --> 00:54:01.337 align:middle line:84%
at least as well, but if
someone said, "What did you

00:54:01.337 --> 00:54:02.940 align:middle line:90%
order a hamburger with?"

00:54:02.940 --> 00:54:04.190 align:middle line:90%
I would be, like, that's fine.

00:54:04.190 --> 00:54:05.357 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah, yeah.

00:54:05.357 --> 00:54:06.437 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, so good point.

00:54:06.437 --> 00:54:08.770 align:middle line:84%
And maybe that's got something
to do with your reaction,

00:54:08.770 --> 00:54:13.060 align:middle line:84%
that you are being maximally
charitable to the person you're

00:54:13.060 --> 00:54:13.670 align:middle line:90%
talking to.

00:54:13.670 --> 00:54:16.128 align:middle line:84%
If somebody said, "What are
you ordering a hamburger-- what

00:54:16.128 --> 00:54:18.070 align:middle line:90%
did you order a hamburger and?"

00:54:18.070 --> 00:54:20.860 align:middle line:84%
you do your best to
pretend that they

00:54:20.860 --> 00:54:22.300 align:middle line:90%
said something grammatical.

00:54:22.300 --> 00:54:23.733 align:middle line:90%
It's clear what they meant.

00:54:23.733 --> 00:54:25.900 align:middle line:84%
And so you're going to
pretend that they said "with"

00:54:25.900 --> 00:54:27.858 align:middle line:90%
instead of "and."

00:54:27.858 --> 00:54:29.650 align:middle line:84%
Maybe there's something
like that going on.

00:54:29.650 --> 00:54:33.580 align:middle line:84%
This is reminding me of
a-- there's work on--

00:54:33.580 --> 00:54:35.180 align:middle line:90%
what's it called?

00:54:35.180 --> 00:54:39.100 align:middle line:84%
I think it's called the Moses
illusion, where people have

00:54:39.100 --> 00:54:43.090 align:middle line:84%
observed that if you ask people
how many animals of each kind

00:54:43.090 --> 00:54:47.020 align:middle line:84%
did Moses take on the
Ark, where if you're

00:54:47.020 --> 00:54:49.540 align:middle line:84%
familiar with the Bible,
you know that Moses did not

00:54:49.540 --> 00:54:51.430 align:middle line:90%
take any animals on the Ark.

00:54:51.430 --> 00:54:54.340 align:middle line:84%
The Ark was Noah,
a different guy.

00:54:54.340 --> 00:54:56.350 align:middle line:84%
Noah was the one who
took animals on the Ark.

00:54:56.350 --> 00:54:59.470 align:middle line:84%
And so the real response to
that is supposed to be, hold on,

00:54:59.470 --> 00:55:01.253 align:middle line:84%
Moses didn't take any
animals on the Ark.

00:55:01.253 --> 00:55:03.670 align:middle line:84%
But that if you do a survey
in which you walk up to people

00:55:03.670 --> 00:55:05.628 align:middle line:84%
at random and say how
many animals of each type

00:55:05.628 --> 00:55:08.020 align:middle line:84%
did Moses take on the Ark,
what they typically do,

00:55:08.020 --> 00:55:10.750 align:middle line:84%
very often, is pretend
that you said Noah.

00:55:10.750 --> 00:55:13.432 align:middle line:84%
So they pretend that
you asked the question

00:55:13.432 --> 00:55:14.890 align:middle line:84%
in a way that makes
sense, and they

00:55:14.890 --> 00:55:16.390 align:middle line:90%
tell you-- they tell you two.

00:55:16.390 --> 00:55:19.495 align:middle line:90%
Which is actually not--

00:55:19.495 --> 00:55:21.370 align:middle line:84%
as I said, I've been
reading the Bible lately

00:55:21.370 --> 00:55:22.787 align:middle line:84%
because of this
Wampanoag project.

00:55:22.787 --> 00:55:25.270 align:middle line:84%
Noah-- I hadn't
remembered this, but Noah

00:55:25.270 --> 00:55:26.890 align:middle line:84%
took two of some
kinds of animals,

00:55:26.890 --> 00:55:28.210 align:middle line:90%
but more of other kinds.

00:55:28.210 --> 00:55:31.450 align:middle line:84%
It had to do with whether they
were edible or not, i think.

00:55:31.450 --> 00:55:35.410 align:middle line:84%
So whether the laws for
what you're allowed to eat

00:55:35.410 --> 00:55:36.850 align:middle line:90%
allowed you to eat them.

00:55:36.850 --> 00:55:38.560 align:middle line:84%
If it was OK to eat
them, he took more,

00:55:38.560 --> 00:55:41.770 align:middle line:84%
I guess so that there
would be some to spare.

00:55:41.770 --> 00:55:42.447 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:55:42.447 --> 00:55:44.530 align:middle line:84%
So I suspect that that's
what's going on with you.

00:55:44.530 --> 00:55:45.905 align:middle line:84%
But it's an
interesting question.

00:55:45.905 --> 00:55:47.990 align:middle line:84%
Actually, your point raises
an interesting point,

00:55:47.990 --> 00:55:50.080 align:middle line:90%
which is that these--

00:55:50.080 --> 00:55:52.570 align:middle line:84%
we're going to talk about
several cases of this kind

00:55:52.570 --> 00:55:57.250 align:middle line:84%
where a wh-phrase seems to
be constrained from moving

00:55:57.250 --> 00:55:59.710 align:middle line:84%
from a particular
place, and the examples

00:55:59.710 --> 00:56:01.810 align:middle line:84%
are always going to be
examples, hopefully,

00:56:01.810 --> 00:56:04.060 align:middle line:84%
where it's not that
you can't figure out

00:56:04.060 --> 00:56:05.380 align:middle line:90%
what the other person means.

00:56:05.380 --> 00:56:07.030 align:middle line:84%
It's not that these
are unthinkable.

00:56:07.030 --> 00:56:09.490 align:middle line:90%
They're just unsayable.

00:56:09.490 --> 00:56:11.320 align:middle line:84%
It's not the right
way to say it.

00:56:11.320 --> 00:56:12.820 align:middle line:84%
Actually, the next
example, I think,

00:56:12.820 --> 00:56:15.560 align:middle line:84%
is particularly
clear in that regard.

00:56:15.560 --> 00:56:16.960 align:middle line:90%
So here are two sentences.

00:56:16.960 --> 00:56:18.943 align:middle line:84%
We've talked a lot
about embedded clauses.

00:56:18.943 --> 00:56:20.860 align:middle line:84%
And I guess when we were
talking about German,

00:56:20.860 --> 00:56:24.040 align:middle line:84%
I touched on the fact that
embedded clauses sometimes have

00:56:24.040 --> 00:56:26.440 align:middle line:90%
it start with complementizers.

00:56:26.440 --> 00:56:28.480 align:middle line:84%
Our English sentences
have pretty reliably

00:56:28.480 --> 00:56:31.240 align:middle line:84%
started with complementizers,
but they don't have to, right?

00:56:31.240 --> 00:56:32.868 align:middle line:84%
So you can say,
"I think that Mary

00:56:32.868 --> 00:56:34.910 align:middle line:84%
should win the election,"
where there's a "that."

00:56:34.910 --> 00:56:37.452 align:middle line:84%
But you can also say, "I think
Mary should win the election."

00:56:37.452 --> 00:56:39.310 align:middle line:90%
There's no complementizer there.

00:56:39.310 --> 00:56:43.600 align:middle line:84%
And then linguists get very
curious about whether, OK--

00:56:43.600 --> 00:56:46.300 align:middle line:84%
I got asked this question
in class at one point.

00:56:46.300 --> 00:56:49.330 align:middle line:84%
Does that mean that I should
draw different trees for these?

00:56:49.330 --> 00:56:51.580 align:middle line:84%
Or does it mean that the
embedded clause should

00:56:51.580 --> 00:56:56.050 align:middle line:84%
be a CP in both cases, and
that C can either be pronounced

00:56:56.050 --> 00:56:58.680 align:middle line:90%
"that" or [SMACKS LIPS].

00:56:58.680 --> 00:56:59.190 align:middle line:90%
Right?

00:56:59.190 --> 00:57:03.450 align:middle line:84%
That C has a pronunciation
where it's just not pronounced.

00:57:03.450 --> 00:57:06.548 align:middle line:84%
And that's a debate
that people have.

00:57:06.548 --> 00:57:07.590 align:middle line:90%
We don't have to have it.

00:57:07.590 --> 00:57:09.480 align:middle line:84%
But let me call your
attention to a fact which

00:57:09.480 --> 00:57:11.438 align:middle line:84%
doesn't hold actually
for all English speakers,

00:57:11.438 --> 00:57:14.125 align:middle line:90%
but for some it does.

00:57:14.125 --> 00:57:16.500 align:middle line:84%
In my English, it's OK to ask
questions like, "Who do you

00:57:16.500 --> 00:57:18.670 align:middle line:90%
think should win the election?"

00:57:18.670 --> 00:57:20.860 align:middle line:84%
But questions like,
"Who do you think

00:57:20.860 --> 00:57:24.164 align:middle line:90%
that should win the election?"

00:57:24.164 --> 00:57:26.740 align:middle line:90%
are no good for me.

00:57:26.740 --> 00:57:29.178 align:middle line:84%
Am I the only person
like that here?

00:57:29.178 --> 00:57:31.095 align:middle line:84%
Is there anybody for
whom these are both fine?

00:57:31.095 --> 00:57:34.700 align:middle line:90%


00:57:34.700 --> 00:57:36.860 align:middle line:84%
Are you communicating
with me in ASL or--

00:57:36.860 --> 00:57:38.810 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I do not know
what the aslerisk--

00:57:38.810 --> 00:57:40.220 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: The
asterisk means it's bad.

00:57:40.220 --> 00:57:41.200 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: The first
one feels fine.

00:57:41.200 --> 00:57:42.670 align:middle line:90%
The second one feels not fine.

00:57:42.670 --> 00:57:43.753 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: OK, good.

00:57:43.753 --> 00:57:45.280 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, so you're like me.

00:57:45.280 --> 00:57:46.750 align:middle line:90%
I mean, interesting, yeah.

00:57:46.750 --> 00:57:49.480 align:middle line:84%
It would be OK if that
were not true, but yeah.

00:57:49.480 --> 00:57:52.030 align:middle line:84%
There are people for whom
both of these are fine.

00:57:52.030 --> 00:57:54.160 align:middle line:84%
So occasionally, when
I'm teaching this class,

00:57:54.160 --> 00:57:56.710 align:middle line:84%
I'll do this slide, and I'll
look out in the audience

00:57:56.710 --> 00:57:59.080 align:middle line:84%
and there'll be one person
who looks really alarmed,

00:57:59.080 --> 00:58:00.627 align:middle line:90%
like they're hallucinating.

00:58:00.627 --> 00:58:02.710 align:middle line:84%
And I'm like, you're from
the Midwest, aren't you?

00:58:02.710 --> 00:58:04.390 align:middle line:90%
And they're like, yes.

00:58:04.390 --> 00:58:07.910 align:middle line:84%
So there's a part of
America where they do this.

00:58:07.910 --> 00:58:10.383 align:middle line:84%
It's something about
living in big, flat areas

00:58:10.383 --> 00:58:11.800 align:middle line:84%
with lots of
agriculture, I guess,

00:58:11.800 --> 00:58:15.530 align:middle line:84%
that it's OK to
say these things.

00:58:15.530 --> 00:58:17.795 align:middle line:84%
There's a lot of interesting
questions about why,

00:58:17.795 --> 00:58:20.170 align:middle line:84%
or what it is that distinguishes
some dialects of English

00:58:20.170 --> 00:58:20.830 align:middle line:90%
from others.

00:58:20.830 --> 00:58:24.520 align:middle line:84%
Because it's not as
if you cover this

00:58:24.520 --> 00:58:26.270 align:middle line:84%
in high school, or
your mom and dad

00:58:26.270 --> 00:58:28.270 align:middle line:84%
punish if you say, "Who
do you think that should

00:58:28.270 --> 00:58:29.140 align:middle line:90%
win the election," right?

00:58:29.140 --> 00:58:30.140 align:middle line:90%
That's not what happens.

00:58:30.140 --> 00:58:34.720 align:middle line:84%
But for most Native speakers of
English, there's this contrast.

00:58:34.720 --> 00:58:37.300 align:middle line:84%
It's called the
that-trace effect.

00:58:37.300 --> 00:58:38.200 align:middle line:90%
Never mind why.

00:58:38.200 --> 00:58:42.340 align:middle line:84%
Well, because the idea is
it's impossible to have

00:58:42.340 --> 00:58:45.550 align:middle line:84%
a "that" immediately
followed by the place

00:58:45.550 --> 00:58:46.780 align:middle line:90%
the wh-phrase came from.

00:58:46.780 --> 00:58:49.150 align:middle line:84%
If you imagine the
wh-phrase when it moves,

00:58:49.150 --> 00:58:51.650 align:middle line:84%
it leaves traces of
itself behind, I guess,

00:58:51.650 --> 00:58:52.460 align:middle line:90%
is the metaphor.

00:58:52.460 --> 00:58:52.960 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:58:52.960 --> 00:58:56.620 align:middle line:90%


00:58:56.620 --> 00:58:59.242 align:middle line:90%
This is specifically about--

00:58:59.242 --> 00:59:01.450 align:middle line:84%
I guess this came out in
the way I just described it.

00:59:01.450 --> 00:59:04.100 align:middle line:84%
It's specifically about
extraction of subjects.

00:59:04.100 --> 00:59:05.650 align:middle line:84%
So it's bad to say,
"Who do you think

00:59:05.650 --> 00:59:07.188 align:middle line:90%
that should win the election?"

00:59:07.188 --> 00:59:08.980 align:middle line:84%
But it's OK to say
things like, "Who do you

00:59:08.980 --> 00:59:10.272 align:middle line:90%
think that we should vote for?"

00:59:10.272 --> 00:59:14.350 align:middle line:84%
So the generalization is not
you can't wh-move past a "that."

00:59:14.350 --> 00:59:16.210 align:middle line:84%
it's OK to wh-move
past a "that."

00:59:16.210 --> 00:59:18.050 align:middle line:84%
It's just not OK to
wh-move past "that"

00:59:18.050 --> 00:59:20.440 align:middle line:90%
which is right next to you.

00:59:20.440 --> 00:59:23.650 align:middle line:84%
And lots of interesting work
on what the heck is going on,

00:59:23.650 --> 00:59:24.700 align:middle line:90%
yeah.

00:59:24.700 --> 00:59:26.312 align:middle line:84%
Again, let me just
urge you, if you

00:59:26.312 --> 00:59:27.770 align:middle line:84%
would like to know
more about this,

00:59:27.770 --> 00:59:29.080 align:middle line:84%
go take more
linguistics classes.

00:59:29.080 --> 00:59:30.400 align:middle line:84%
This is something
linguists work on,

00:59:30.400 --> 00:59:31.942 align:middle line:84%
trying to figure
out what's going on.

00:59:31.942 --> 00:59:36.070 align:middle line:90%


00:59:36.070 --> 00:59:39.400 align:middle line:84%
Another example of movement
not being possible.

00:59:39.400 --> 00:59:41.530 align:middle line:84%
We actually had an
example like this earlier.

00:59:41.530 --> 00:59:44.260 align:middle line:84%
All of our CPs so far,
or most of our CPs

00:59:44.260 --> 00:59:46.102 align:middle line:84%
so far have been
complements of verbs.

00:59:46.102 --> 00:59:47.560 align:middle line:84%
So we've said things
like, "I think

00:59:47.560 --> 00:59:49.353 align:middle line:84%
that she should win
the election," where

00:59:49.353 --> 00:59:50.770 align:middle line:84%
"that she should
win the election"

00:59:50.770 --> 00:59:52.210 align:middle line:90%
is the complement of "think."

00:59:52.210 --> 00:59:55.330 align:middle line:84%
Or "people believe that the
moon is made of green cheese,"

00:59:55.330 --> 00:59:59.380 align:middle line:84%
where "believe" takes as its
sister, its complement, a CP,

00:59:59.380 --> 01:00:01.540 align:middle line:84%
"that the moon is
made of green cheese."

01:00:01.540 --> 01:00:03.880 align:middle line:84%
It's also possible for
CPs to be subjects,

01:00:03.880 --> 01:00:05.980 align:middle line:84%
as in "That the moon
is made of green cheese

01:00:05.980 --> 01:00:07.120 align:middle line:90%
is widely believed."

01:00:07.120 --> 01:00:10.210 align:middle line:90%
That's false, but grammatical.

01:00:10.210 --> 01:00:11.810 align:middle line:90%
People agree?

01:00:11.810 --> 01:00:12.310 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:00:12.310 --> 01:00:15.160 align:middle line:84%
It's not the most natural
thing I've said today,

01:00:15.160 --> 01:00:18.320 align:middle line:90%
but it's grammatical.

01:00:18.320 --> 01:00:23.350 align:middle line:84%
Now, so there's a tree
for "That the moon is

01:00:23.350 --> 01:00:25.360 align:middle line:84%
made of green cheese
is widely believed."

01:00:25.360 --> 01:00:28.090 align:middle line:84%
We've got a CP, which is sitting
in the specifier of a TP.

01:00:28.090 --> 01:00:30.850 align:middle line:84%
It's the subject of
"is widely believed."

01:00:30.850 --> 01:00:35.470 align:middle line:90%


01:00:35.470 --> 01:00:36.940 align:middle line:90%
Now let's do some wh-movement.

01:00:36.940 --> 01:00:39.190 align:middle line:84%
It's OK to ask questions
like, "What do people believe

01:00:39.190 --> 01:00:40.870 align:middle line:90%
that the moon is made of?"

01:00:40.870 --> 01:00:44.140 align:middle line:84%
so we took that first
sentence and we wh-moved.

01:00:44.140 --> 01:00:46.557 align:middle line:84%
We turned "green cheese"
into "what" and wh-moved it.

01:00:46.557 --> 01:00:48.640 align:middle line:84%
"What do people believe
that the moon is made of?"

01:00:48.640 --> 01:00:49.890 align:middle line:90%
Fine.

01:00:49.890 --> 01:00:52.430 align:middle line:84%
But you can see
where this is going.

01:00:52.430 --> 01:00:57.770 align:middle line:84%
"What is that the moon is
made of widely believed?"

01:00:57.770 --> 01:00:59.720 align:middle line:90%
Ow, yeah?

01:00:59.720 --> 01:01:01.130 align:middle line:90%
So, you know.

01:01:01.130 --> 01:01:03.980 align:middle line:84%
"That the moon is made of green
cheese is widely believed," OK,

01:01:03.980 --> 01:01:05.780 align:middle line:84%
that's possibly
a little awkward.

01:01:05.780 --> 01:01:09.720 align:middle line:84%
Not something I'm likely to
say in casual conversation.

01:01:09.720 --> 01:01:12.770 align:middle line:84%
But the last example
is just word salad,

01:01:12.770 --> 01:01:13.970 align:middle line:90%
to use the technical term.

01:01:13.970 --> 01:01:14.720 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:01:14.720 --> 01:01:18.200 align:middle line:84%
It's very unclear what the
heck it's supposed to mean.

01:01:18.200 --> 01:01:19.550 align:middle line:90%
Do people agree?

01:01:19.550 --> 01:01:22.880 align:middle line:84%
Again, I'm reporting
my memory of judgments

01:01:22.880 --> 01:01:26.060 align:middle line:84%
that I had when I was
young and carefree,

01:01:26.060 --> 01:01:27.680 align:middle line:84%
but I'm pretty
sure this is true.

01:01:27.680 --> 01:01:29.370 align:middle line:90%
You just can't say these things.

01:01:29.370 --> 01:01:30.710 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:01:30.710 --> 01:01:31.490 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:01:31.490 --> 01:01:34.010 align:middle line:84%
All right, so these
kinds of examples

01:01:34.010 --> 01:01:38.540 align:middle line:84%
are all just meant to show you
there are some kinds of things

01:01:38.540 --> 01:01:40.410 align:middle line:90%
that you can't wh-move out of.

01:01:40.410 --> 01:01:43.220 align:middle line:84%
So if you coordinate two things,
you can't move one of them.

01:01:43.220 --> 01:01:45.838 align:middle line:84%
The that-trace effect is
a condition on extraction

01:01:45.838 --> 01:01:46.880 align:middle line:90%
of subjects specifically.

01:01:46.880 --> 01:01:50.850 align:middle line:84%
You can't move those if there's
a "that" right to their left.

01:01:50.850 --> 01:01:53.030 align:middle line:84%
And if you have
a clause which is

01:01:53.030 --> 01:01:56.090 align:middle line:84%
a subject, like "that the
moon is made of green cheese

01:01:56.090 --> 01:01:59.720 align:middle line:84%
is widely believed," you
can't wh-extract out of that.

01:01:59.720 --> 01:02:01.400 align:middle line:84%
There's a metaphor
that's often used

01:02:01.400 --> 01:02:02.810 align:middle line:90%
for these kinds of restrictions.

01:02:02.810 --> 01:02:06.870 align:middle line:84%
The things that you can't move
out of are called islands.

01:02:06.870 --> 01:02:10.880 align:middle line:84%
So this last one I've identified
for you is a subject island.

01:02:10.880 --> 01:02:14.060 align:middle line:84%
So I guess the idea
is supposed to be that

01:02:14.060 --> 01:02:17.180 align:middle line:84%
if you're a wh-phrase
and you're on an island,

01:02:17.180 --> 01:02:19.190 align:middle line:90%
wh-phrases cannot swim.

01:02:19.190 --> 01:02:22.047 align:middle line:84%
There is no boat,
there is no bridge.

01:02:22.047 --> 01:02:23.630 align:middle line:84%
If you're on an
island, you're doomed.

01:02:23.630 --> 01:02:26.000 align:middle line:90%
You cannot get out.

01:02:26.000 --> 01:02:27.510 align:middle line:90%
You're stuck.

01:02:27.510 --> 01:02:31.400 align:middle line:84%
So there's a thriving
literature in syntax

01:02:31.400 --> 01:02:33.770 align:middle line:84%
in which people
identify islands and try

01:02:33.770 --> 01:02:37.145 align:middle line:84%
to figure out why the things
are islands that are islands.

01:02:37.145 --> 01:02:39.770 align:middle line:84%
We hope that this will teach us
some things about the mechanics

01:02:39.770 --> 01:02:40.640 align:middle line:90%
of extraction.

01:02:40.640 --> 01:02:41.690 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:02:41.690 --> 01:02:44.960 align:middle line:84%
I want to show you a particular
class of island effects,

01:02:44.960 --> 01:02:48.350 align:middle line:84%
or cases where
movement is impossible,

01:02:48.350 --> 01:02:50.300 align:middle line:84%
and show you a little
bit of the work

01:02:50.300 --> 01:02:53.150 align:middle line:84%
that people have done to try
to make some progress on why

01:02:53.150 --> 01:02:56.300 align:middle line:84%
these particular things
block extraction.

01:02:56.300 --> 01:02:59.360 align:middle line:84%
I think we have time to at
least get started on this,

01:02:59.360 --> 01:03:03.363 align:middle line:84%
and then we might have
to finish it next time.

01:03:03.363 --> 01:03:05.780 align:middle line:84%
So there are a number of kinds
of restrictions on movement

01:03:05.780 --> 01:03:08.780 align:middle line:84%
that could be unified, and I'll
show these to you in a second.

01:03:08.780 --> 01:03:12.323 align:middle line:84%
They could be unified into
a single condition, which

01:03:12.323 --> 01:03:13.490 align:middle line:90%
we could call shortest move.

01:03:13.490 --> 01:03:16.610 align:middle line:84%
It says if you have a choice
between two different movement

01:03:16.610 --> 01:03:19.665 align:middle line:84%
operations, you should pick
the one that's shorter.

01:03:19.665 --> 01:03:22.040 align:middle line:84%
And people are kind of excited
by that, because it sounds

01:03:22.040 --> 01:03:24.740 align:middle line:90%
like cognitively plausible.

01:03:24.740 --> 01:03:27.530 align:middle line:84%
If you're trying to decide
between movement operations,

01:03:27.530 --> 01:03:29.630 align:middle line:84%
you should pick a short
one over a long one.

01:03:29.630 --> 01:03:32.340 align:middle line:90%


01:03:32.340 --> 01:03:34.250 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to show you
a definition of "short"

01:03:34.250 --> 01:03:35.930 align:middle line:90%
in a second.

01:03:35.930 --> 01:03:39.950 align:middle line:84%
Please don't go tattoo this
on your arm or anything.

01:03:39.950 --> 01:03:42.513 align:middle line:84%
There's no-- it's not all that
important that we define it

01:03:42.513 --> 01:03:44.180 align:middle line:84%
carefully for the
purposes of what we're

01:03:44.180 --> 01:03:45.740 align:middle line:90%
going to do in this class.

01:03:45.740 --> 01:03:47.655 align:middle line:84%
In theoretical
work on this topic,

01:03:47.655 --> 01:03:49.280 align:middle line:84%
it is important to
define it carefully,

01:03:49.280 --> 01:03:50.630 align:middle line:84%
and there's work on
trying to figure out

01:03:50.630 --> 01:03:51.980 align:middle line:90%
exactly how to define it.

01:03:51.980 --> 01:03:53.510 align:middle line:90%
But here's one definition.

01:03:53.510 --> 01:03:56.570 align:middle line:90%
You could say, take the path--

01:03:56.570 --> 01:03:58.430 align:middle line:84%
take a movement
operation, and we'll

01:03:58.430 --> 01:04:02.570 align:middle line:84%
consider-- let me show
you a movement operation.

01:04:02.570 --> 01:04:04.070 align:middle line:84%
So here's a movement
operation where

01:04:04.070 --> 01:04:06.770 align:middle line:84%
we took "with chopsticks"
in this V2 example

01:04:06.770 --> 01:04:10.100 align:middle line:84%
and we moved it out of
the TP and into the C.

01:04:10.100 --> 01:04:12.140 align:middle line:84%
And we'll talk about
the path of a movement,

01:04:12.140 --> 01:04:15.410 align:middle line:84%
and that'll be the set
of nodes that dominate

01:04:15.410 --> 01:04:17.180 align:middle line:84%
the original
position, the position

01:04:17.180 --> 01:04:20.280 align:middle line:84%
you moved out of, and don't
dominate the landing site.

01:04:20.280 --> 01:04:24.030 align:middle line:84%
So in this particular
case of movement,

01:04:24.030 --> 01:04:26.062 align:middle line:84%
this CP dominates
the landing site.

01:04:26.062 --> 01:04:27.770 align:middle line:84%
But then there are a
bunch of other nodes

01:04:27.770 --> 01:04:29.395 align:middle line:84%
like this one and
this one, and a bunch

01:04:29.395 --> 01:04:32.990 align:middle line:84%
of other ones inside this TP,
that dominate this XP here.

01:04:32.990 --> 01:04:36.980 align:middle line:84%
And those are what we'll
refer to as the path.

01:04:36.980 --> 01:04:39.890 align:middle line:84%
And to claim that a move has
to be as short as possible

01:04:39.890 --> 01:04:42.110 align:middle line:84%
is to claim that you
want the path to be,

01:04:42.110 --> 01:04:44.700 align:middle line:90%
well, as small as possible.

01:04:44.700 --> 01:04:46.308 align:middle line:84%
And there's some
interesting work

01:04:46.308 --> 01:04:47.850 align:middle line:84%
on trying to figure
out what happens.

01:04:47.850 --> 01:04:50.210 align:middle line:84%
So in the cases I'm
going to show you,

01:04:50.210 --> 01:04:52.670 align:middle line:84%
the two paths are in
a subset relation.

01:04:52.670 --> 01:04:55.130 align:middle line:84%
One of them just
contains a subset

01:04:55.130 --> 01:04:57.200 align:middle line:84%
of the nodes that
are in the other one.

01:04:57.200 --> 01:04:58.160 align:middle line:84%
And there's an
interesting question

01:04:58.160 --> 01:04:59.827 align:middle line:84%
about what happens
if they just overlap?

01:04:59.827 --> 01:05:01.100 align:middle line:90%
Do you actually count nodes?

01:05:01.100 --> 01:05:02.642 align:middle line:84%
People sort of hope
not, and it looks

01:05:02.642 --> 01:05:04.460 align:middle line:90%
like the answer might be no.

01:05:04.460 --> 01:05:06.770 align:middle line:84%
We won't look at any of
their relevant cases today.

01:05:06.770 --> 01:05:08.990 align:middle line:84%
So movement A is going to
be shorter than movement B

01:05:08.990 --> 01:05:11.360 align:middle line:84%
if the path of A contains
a smaller set of nodes

01:05:11.360 --> 01:05:13.850 align:middle line:84%
than the path of B. I'll
show you some trees that

01:05:13.850 --> 01:05:15.820 align:middle line:90%
will hopefully make that clear.

01:05:15.820 --> 01:05:18.790 align:middle line:84%
So let me show you a case
where shortest move is useful,

01:05:18.790 --> 01:05:21.220 align:middle line:84%
something called the
head movement constraint.

01:05:21.220 --> 01:05:23.020 align:middle line:84%
It was invented by
Lisa Travis, who's

01:05:23.020 --> 01:05:26.500 align:middle line:84%
a syntactician who now teaches
at McGill University up

01:05:26.500 --> 01:05:28.300 align:middle line:90%
in Montreal.

01:05:28.300 --> 01:05:30.040 align:middle line:84%
The head movement
constraint says this.

01:05:30.040 --> 01:05:31.373 align:middle line:90%
Here's a case of head movement--

01:05:31.373 --> 01:05:32.950 align:middle line:90%
I showed it to you before--

01:05:32.950 --> 01:05:35.770 align:middle line:84%
where we've taken what's in
T and we've moved it into C.

01:05:35.770 --> 01:05:38.380 align:middle line:84%
So "will Mary type novels,"
we've taken the auxiliary

01:05:38.380 --> 01:05:40.210 align:middle line:90%
and we've moved it into C.

01:05:40.210 --> 01:05:42.880 align:middle line:84%
Suppose instead we
were to take the verb

01:05:42.880 --> 01:05:46.010 align:middle line:84%
and move it into C.
Well, we would end up

01:05:46.010 --> 01:05:48.380 align:middle line:84%
with "type Mary
will novels," which

01:05:48.380 --> 01:05:53.090 align:middle line:84%
is not the way you ask yes-no
questions in English, no.

01:05:53.090 --> 01:05:54.890 align:middle line:84%
And in fact, this
generally seems

01:05:54.890 --> 01:05:58.110 align:middle line:84%
to be true, that if you are
going to do head movement--

01:05:58.110 --> 01:06:00.350 align:middle line:84%
so you're going to
move some head into C--

01:06:00.350 --> 01:06:02.930 align:middle line:84%
the head that you move
is the higher head.

01:06:02.930 --> 01:06:03.650 align:middle line:90%
It's "will."

01:06:03.650 --> 01:06:04.820 align:middle line:90%
It's not the lower head.

01:06:04.820 --> 01:06:07.735 align:middle line:90%
It's not V.

01:06:07.735 --> 01:06:11.650 align:middle line:84%
The path-- so if we're talking
about this in terms of paths,

01:06:11.650 --> 01:06:15.910 align:middle line:84%
the path from I to C--
sorry, from T to C.

01:06:15.910 --> 01:06:18.070 align:middle line:84%
I have to go through and
get rid of these I's.

01:06:18.070 --> 01:06:23.650 align:middle line:84%
This I, again, is an old name
for T. The path from T, from

01:06:23.650 --> 01:06:27.160 align:middle line:84%
"will," up into C
consists of T bar and TP.

01:06:27.160 --> 01:06:29.290 align:middle line:84%
Those are the nodes that
dominate T and don't

01:06:29.290 --> 01:06:30.760 align:middle line:90%
dominate C.

01:06:30.760 --> 01:06:33.100 align:middle line:84%
But the path from
V to C consists

01:06:33.100 --> 01:06:35.890 align:middle line:90%
of VP and T bar and TP.

01:06:35.890 --> 01:06:36.790 align:middle line:90%
Do people see that?

01:06:36.790 --> 01:06:38.207 align:middle line:84%
Should I draw that
tree again down

01:06:38.207 --> 01:06:41.030 align:middle line:84%
here, and I can circle nodes
and we can look at them?

01:06:41.030 --> 01:06:43.030 align:middle line:84%
So when we're
talking about paths--

01:06:43.030 --> 01:06:47.590 align:middle line:84%
again, this is one way of
measuring lengths of movement

01:06:47.590 --> 01:06:51.490 align:middle line:84%
operations-- the
path from V to C

01:06:51.490 --> 01:06:54.130 align:middle line:84%
is the set of nodes that
dominate V, that don't dominate

01:06:54.130 --> 01:06:58.030 align:middle line:84%
C. So those nodes are the VP,
which immediately dominates A,

01:06:58.030 --> 01:07:00.430 align:middle line:84%
and the T bar, which
dominates the VP,

01:07:00.430 --> 01:07:04.510 align:middle line:84%
and the T which dominates the T
bar, but not the CP, which also

01:07:04.510 --> 01:07:06.520 align:middle line:84%
dominates the C. So
we're looking just

01:07:06.520 --> 01:07:09.160 align:middle line:84%
at the nodes which dominate
the place the movement started

01:07:09.160 --> 01:07:11.480 align:middle line:84%
and don't dominate the
place that it lands.

01:07:11.480 --> 01:07:14.380 align:middle line:90%
So that's the path from V to C.

01:07:14.380 --> 01:07:16.960 align:middle line:84%
And that path is a superset
of the path for movement

01:07:16.960 --> 01:07:19.750 align:middle line:84%
from T to C, because
VP is in that path,

01:07:19.750 --> 01:07:23.320 align:middle line:84%
but it's not in the path from T
to C. Does everybody see that?

01:07:23.320 --> 01:07:24.860 align:middle line:90%
Is that clear?

01:07:24.860 --> 01:07:25.360 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:07:25.360 --> 01:07:29.110 align:middle line:84%
We're mostly going to know
short when we see it, right?

01:07:29.110 --> 01:07:31.203 align:middle line:84%
So another way to
say all of this

01:07:31.203 --> 01:07:32.620 align:middle line:84%
would have been
to say, hey, look,

01:07:32.620 --> 01:07:35.770 align:middle line:84%
the arrow that connects T to C
is shorter than the arrow that

01:07:35.770 --> 01:07:37.750 align:middle line:90%
connects V to C. There, right?

01:07:37.750 --> 01:07:43.360 align:middle line:84%
Or, hey, look, TC
commands V. So every node

01:07:43.360 --> 01:07:46.510 align:middle line:84%
that dominates T dominates V.
That would have done it too.

01:07:46.510 --> 01:07:48.833 align:middle line:84%
There are various ways
to do it, but this talk

01:07:48.833 --> 01:07:51.250 align:middle line:84%
in terms of paths, this is one
way people do it sometimes.

01:07:51.250 --> 01:07:54.560 align:middle line:90%


01:07:54.560 --> 01:07:57.655 align:middle line:84%
So head movement constraint
is one of the sub cases.

01:07:57.655 --> 01:08:00.030 align:middle line:84%
So the head movement constraint
was invented decades ago.

01:08:00.030 --> 01:08:04.400 align:middle line:84%
It's one of the cases that was
folded into this general notion

01:08:04.400 --> 01:08:05.750 align:middle line:90%
of shortest move.

01:08:05.750 --> 01:08:08.000 align:middle line:84%
When you have a choice between
things you could move--

01:08:08.000 --> 01:08:10.160 align:middle line:84%
in this case, you're
choosing between T and V--

01:08:10.160 --> 01:08:13.310 align:middle line:84%
you have to choose T. You can't
choose V. It's what the head

01:08:13.310 --> 01:08:15.400 align:middle line:90%
movement constraint says.

01:08:15.400 --> 01:08:18.050 align:middle line:90%
Another example.

01:08:18.050 --> 01:08:20.439 align:middle line:84%
We talked a little bit
about multiple-wh questions,

01:08:20.439 --> 01:08:22.750 align:middle line:84%
questions like
"Who bought what?"

01:08:22.750 --> 01:08:24.700 align:middle line:90%
And speakers vary.

01:08:24.700 --> 01:08:27.700 align:middle line:84%
Actually, I'd be interested
to hear how you guys feel--

01:08:27.700 --> 01:08:28.479 align:middle line:90%
how you all feel.

01:08:28.479 --> 01:08:34.260 align:middle line:84%
I prefer "Who bought what?"
to "What did who buy?"

01:08:34.260 --> 01:08:36.990 align:middle line:90%
or "What did you give to whom?"

01:08:36.990 --> 01:08:40.720 align:middle line:84%
sounds better to me than
"Who did you give what to?"

01:08:40.720 --> 01:08:43.100 align:middle line:84%
Do people agree
with me about that?

01:08:43.100 --> 01:08:45.810 align:middle line:90%
Does that sound true?

01:08:45.810 --> 01:08:47.630 align:middle line:84%
I saw some of you
nodding encouragingly

01:08:47.630 --> 01:08:49.208 align:middle line:90%
as I was doing this.

01:08:49.208 --> 01:08:50.750 align:middle line:84%
Maybe you just wanted
to be friendly.

01:08:50.750 --> 01:08:51.620 align:middle line:90%
I appreciate that.

01:08:51.620 --> 01:08:52.520 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:08:52.520 --> 01:08:54.545 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I prefer
the first options,

01:08:54.545 --> 01:08:56.420 align:middle line:84%
but I don't think that
the second options are

01:08:56.420 --> 01:08:57.319 align:middle line:90%
ungrammatical.

01:08:57.319 --> 01:08:58.310 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

01:08:58.310 --> 01:09:00.560 align:middle line:84%
So this is different from
the head movement constraint

01:09:00.560 --> 01:09:01.649 align:middle line:90%
in at least that regard.

01:09:01.649 --> 01:09:03.899 align:middle line:84%
The head movement constraint--
if you violate the head

01:09:03.899 --> 01:09:06.066 align:middle line:84%
movement constraint, you've
done something very bad.

01:09:06.066 --> 01:09:08.300 align:middle line:84%
It's no longer clear
what you meant, yeah.

01:09:08.300 --> 01:09:11.149 align:middle line:84%
Whereas if I say,
"What did who buy?"

01:09:11.149 --> 01:09:12.359 align:middle line:90%
people will look at me funny.

01:09:12.359 --> 01:09:13.910 align:middle line:84%
But people look at
me funny anyway.

01:09:13.910 --> 01:09:16.760 align:middle line:84%
That's kind of what
life is like, yeah?

01:09:16.760 --> 01:09:19.390 align:middle line:90%


01:09:19.390 --> 01:09:21.130 align:middle line:84%
Still, I also
agree with Kateryna

01:09:21.130 --> 01:09:23.020 align:middle line:84%
that the first
examples are better

01:09:23.020 --> 01:09:24.770 align:middle line:90%
than the second examples.

01:09:24.770 --> 01:09:28.569 align:middle line:84%
This is sometimes called
superiority, the phenomenon

01:09:28.569 --> 01:09:30.760 align:middle line:84%
that if you have two
wh-phrases and you're

01:09:30.760 --> 01:09:33.130 align:middle line:84%
trying to pick which one to
move, there's a preference.

01:09:33.130 --> 01:09:35.380 align:middle line:84%
As Kateryna, I think,
accurately says,

01:09:35.380 --> 01:09:37.279 align:middle line:84%
it's not a hugely
strong preference.

01:09:37.279 --> 01:09:41.770 align:middle line:84%
But it's a preference for moving
the higher one, the first one.

01:09:41.770 --> 01:09:44.029 align:middle line:84%
And this is kind of like the
head movement constraint,

01:09:44.029 --> 01:09:46.750 align:middle line:84%
and people have subsumed
it under shortest move.

01:09:46.750 --> 01:09:48.740 align:middle line:84%
You've got a choice
between two things to move.

01:09:48.740 --> 01:09:51.310 align:middle line:84%
You ought to move the higher
one, the one where for which

01:09:51.310 --> 01:09:52.840 align:middle line:90%
the movement will be shorter.

01:09:52.840 --> 01:09:53.680 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:09:53.680 --> 01:09:55.472 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Could this
be related in some way

01:09:55.472 --> 01:09:58.570 align:middle line:84%
to what the most important
part of the sentence is?

01:09:58.570 --> 01:10:00.580 align:middle line:84%
Like, if you hear
someone say something

01:10:00.580 --> 01:10:02.330 align:middle line:84%
like "We're buying
something," and they're

01:10:02.330 --> 01:10:05.260 align:middle line:84%
buying this really
crazy thing, you

01:10:05.260 --> 01:10:07.110 align:middle line:90%
could say, "What did you buy?"

01:10:07.110 --> 01:10:08.200 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Ah, yes.

01:10:08.200 --> 01:10:11.230 align:middle line:90%


01:10:11.230 --> 01:10:11.890 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:10:11.890 --> 01:10:12.520 align:middle line:90%
Let's see now.

01:10:12.520 --> 01:10:17.690 align:middle line:90%


01:10:17.690 --> 01:10:19.050 align:middle line:90%
Huh.

01:10:19.050 --> 01:10:21.468 align:middle line:84%
I'm sorry, I'm going to do
an old professor's trick

01:10:21.468 --> 01:10:23.760 align:middle line:84%
and talk about something
different from what you talked

01:10:23.760 --> 01:10:27.278 align:middle line:84%
about, because what you said
reminded me of something else.

01:10:27.278 --> 01:10:29.070 align:middle line:84%
And then I'm going to
talk about that thing

01:10:29.070 --> 01:10:30.300 align:middle line:84%
hopefully long
enough so that you'll

01:10:30.300 --> 01:10:31.425 align:middle line:90%
forget your original point.

01:10:31.425 --> 01:10:32.670 align:middle line:90%
That's the goal.

01:10:32.670 --> 01:10:35.910 align:middle line:84%
So your job is to stop me
from succeeding at this.

01:10:35.910 --> 01:10:39.090 align:middle line:84%
But what I thought you were
going to say went like this.

01:10:39.090 --> 01:10:41.770 align:middle line:84%
There is a kind of wh-question--
we've talked about it.

01:10:41.770 --> 01:10:45.510 align:middle line:84%
It's called an echo
question, where I just

01:10:45.510 --> 01:10:47.610 align:middle line:84%
repeat what you
said, except that I

01:10:47.610 --> 01:10:50.130 align:middle line:84%
substitute in a wh-phrase
which is in-situ.

01:10:50.130 --> 01:10:53.880 align:middle line:84%
So if you say "Mary just bought
a motorcycle," I can say,

01:10:53.880 --> 01:10:55.830 align:middle line:90%
"Mary bought what?"

01:10:55.830 --> 01:10:58.530 align:middle line:84%
And similarly, if you
say, "Mary just bought

01:10:58.530 --> 01:11:01.290 align:middle line:84%
a motorcycle," I can say,
"Who bought a motorcycle?"

01:11:01.290 --> 01:11:03.960 align:middle line:84%
And because that's at the
beginning of the sentence,

01:11:03.960 --> 01:11:07.000 align:middle line:84%
it's hard to know whether
that's an echo question or not.

01:11:07.000 --> 01:11:10.440 align:middle line:84%
But at least it's
possible that it is.

01:11:10.440 --> 01:11:12.870 align:middle line:84%
And I can do that either
because it's hard to believe--

01:11:12.870 --> 01:11:15.412 align:middle line:84%
there are various people I could
believe bought a motorcycle,

01:11:15.412 --> 01:11:18.600 align:middle line:84%
but not Mary, we all know Mary
would never buy a motorcycle--

01:11:18.600 --> 01:11:20.268 align:middle line:84%
or I could do it
because I didn't

01:11:20.268 --> 01:11:21.810 align:middle line:84%
hear the first word
of your sentence,

01:11:21.810 --> 01:11:24.270 align:middle line:84%
like the phone connection
is bad or we're

01:11:24.270 --> 01:11:26.772 align:middle line:84%
talking on the T or whatever,
and there was a loud noise

01:11:26.772 --> 01:11:29.230 align:middle line:84%
right when you said "Mary,"
and so I didn't hear that part.

01:11:29.230 --> 01:11:31.665 align:middle line:84%
And I can say, "Who
bought a motorcycle?"

01:11:31.665 --> 01:11:34.290 align:middle line:84%
Not because I'm astonished, but
because I missed the first part

01:11:34.290 --> 01:11:36.660 align:middle line:90%
of your sentence.

01:11:36.660 --> 01:11:39.990 align:middle line:84%
And "What did who
buy?" that string,

01:11:39.990 --> 01:11:44.460 align:middle line:84%
I think it's possible to have an
echo question with wh-movement

01:11:44.460 --> 01:11:45.450 align:middle line:90%
in it.

01:11:45.450 --> 01:11:50.670 align:middle line:84%
So if you've just asked
me, "What did Mary buy?"

01:11:50.670 --> 01:11:55.390 align:middle line:84%
and I didn't hear you say
"Mary" because the line was bad.

01:11:55.390 --> 01:11:57.660 align:middle line:84%
So you said, "What did
[IMITATING STATIC] buy?"

01:11:57.660 --> 01:12:01.370 align:middle line:90%
I can say, "What did who buy?"

01:12:01.370 --> 01:12:05.060 align:middle line:84%
Which resembles what
you were talking about,

01:12:05.060 --> 01:12:06.510 align:middle line:90%
but it's not quite the same.

01:12:06.510 --> 01:12:11.540 align:middle line:84%
You wanted-- so that's a place
where my two wh-phrases have

01:12:11.540 --> 01:12:14.150 align:middle line:90%
different goals.

01:12:14.150 --> 01:12:16.820 align:middle line:90%
When I say, "What did who buy?"

01:12:16.820 --> 01:12:18.830 align:middle line:84%
I'm really asking you
the question "who,"

01:12:18.830 --> 01:12:20.893 align:middle line:90%
and my "what" is quoting you.

01:12:20.893 --> 01:12:23.060 align:middle line:84%
What you said was "What did
[IMITATING STATIC] buy?"

01:12:23.060 --> 01:12:24.920 align:middle line:90%
right.

01:12:24.920 --> 01:12:27.290 align:middle line:84%
That's not what you said,
but that's what I heard.

01:12:27.290 --> 01:12:30.080 align:middle line:84%
And so I'm doing
an echo question

01:12:30.080 --> 01:12:31.380 align:middle line:90%
about your wh-question.

01:12:31.380 --> 01:12:31.880 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:12:31.880 --> 01:12:34.430 align:middle line:90%
My real question is about "who."

01:12:34.430 --> 01:12:39.770 align:middle line:84%
Your idea was that if I
ask, "What did who buy?"

01:12:39.770 --> 01:12:40.960 align:middle line:90%
"What did who buy?"

01:12:40.960 --> 01:12:42.710 align:middle line:84%
No, your question was,
"What did who buy?"

01:12:42.710 --> 01:12:43.490 align:middle line:90%
What was your question?

01:12:43.490 --> 01:12:44.900 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Like, if you're
more interested in the what

01:12:44.900 --> 01:12:45.710 align:middle line:90%
than who.

01:12:45.710 --> 01:12:46.460 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: If I'm
more interested in the what

01:12:46.460 --> 01:12:47.480 align:middle line:90%
than the who.

01:12:47.480 --> 01:12:51.080 align:middle line:90%


01:12:51.080 --> 01:12:54.210 align:middle line:90%
So this is related--

01:12:54.210 --> 01:12:54.710 align:middle line:90%
yeah.

01:12:54.710 --> 01:12:57.530 align:middle line:90%


01:12:57.530 --> 01:12:59.050 align:middle line:90%
This is related to other things.

01:12:59.050 --> 01:13:01.490 align:middle line:84%
Let me see, what's a
good example of this?

01:13:01.490 --> 01:13:07.985 align:middle line:84%
It's a thing about
wh-answers to wh-questions.

01:13:07.985 --> 01:13:09.985 align:middle line:84%
if I ask you a question
like, "Who bought what?"

01:13:09.985 --> 01:13:12.730 align:middle line:90%


01:13:12.730 --> 01:13:16.840 align:middle line:84%
I want a complete
list of people.

01:13:16.840 --> 01:13:20.980 align:middle line:84%
I want my answer organized
as a complete list of people,

01:13:20.980 --> 01:13:25.960 align:middle line:84%
together with the
things that they bought.

01:13:25.960 --> 01:13:31.690 align:middle line:84%
Whereas if I make myself
ask you, "What did who buy?"

01:13:31.690 --> 01:13:35.590 align:middle line:84%
what I want is a
complete list of things

01:13:35.590 --> 01:13:38.290 align:middle line:84%
organized by things
together with the people who

01:13:38.290 --> 01:13:39.400 align:middle line:90%
bought them.

01:13:39.400 --> 01:13:42.280 align:middle line:84%
And there's some debate
about whether-- so

01:13:42.280 --> 01:13:44.860 align:middle line:84%
under normal
circumstances, wh-questions

01:13:44.860 --> 01:13:47.050 align:middle line:90%
are supposed to be exhaustive.

01:13:47.050 --> 01:13:49.120 align:middle line:84%
Answers are supposed to
be exhaustive, that is.

01:13:49.120 --> 01:13:55.450 align:middle line:84%
If I ask you, "What
did you eat yesterday?"

01:13:55.450 --> 01:13:59.110 align:middle line:84%
you're supposed to give me a
more or less complete list.

01:13:59.110 --> 01:14:02.290 align:middle line:84%
If you leave out all the
desserts and I'm your doctor,

01:14:02.290 --> 01:14:04.180 align:middle line:84%
then you are you're
doing something wrong.

01:14:04.180 --> 01:14:07.120 align:middle line:84%
You're supposed to
tell me everything.

01:14:07.120 --> 01:14:09.550 align:middle line:84%
There are circumstances
under which it clearly isn't

01:14:09.550 --> 01:14:10.790 align:middle line:90%
supposed to be exhaustive.

01:14:10.790 --> 01:14:13.180 align:middle line:84%
So if I ask you a question
like, "Where can I

01:14:13.180 --> 01:14:15.192 align:middle line:84%
buy a foreign
newspaper around here?"

01:14:15.192 --> 01:14:16.900 align:middle line:84%
you don't have to give
me a complete list

01:14:16.900 --> 01:14:19.233 align:middle line:84%
of the places I can buy a
foreign newspaper around here.

01:14:19.233 --> 01:14:22.780 align:middle line:84%
You just have to tell me
a place where I can go.

01:14:22.780 --> 01:14:24.700 align:middle line:90%
"Harvard Square," you can say.

01:14:24.700 --> 01:14:26.680 align:middle line:90%
Maybe that's where I can go.

01:14:26.680 --> 01:14:29.470 align:middle line:84%
And with multiple
wh-questions, there's

01:14:29.470 --> 01:14:32.410 align:middle line:84%
some debate about whether
the exhaustivity requirement,

01:14:32.410 --> 01:14:34.960 align:middle line:84%
to the extent that it ever
holds with wh-questions,

01:14:34.960 --> 01:14:38.140 align:middle line:84%
maybe only holds of the one
that you move and not of the one

01:14:38.140 --> 01:14:39.640 align:middle line:90%
that you've left in situ.

01:14:39.640 --> 01:14:42.220 align:middle line:84%
That's a position people have
held anyway, that there's

01:14:42.220 --> 01:14:44.480 align:middle line:90%
a difference between the two.

01:14:44.480 --> 01:14:46.210 align:middle line:84%
And so your question
about importance

01:14:46.210 --> 01:14:47.740 align:middle line:90%
might be getting at that, right?

01:14:47.740 --> 01:14:50.080 align:middle line:84%
It's like, I want this
one completely exhausted,

01:14:50.080 --> 01:14:51.010 align:middle line:90%
and then--

01:14:51.010 --> 01:14:53.410 align:middle line:84%
yes, I want every member of
it connected to something

01:14:53.410 --> 01:14:54.820 align:middle line:84%
in the other group,
but you don't

01:14:54.820 --> 01:14:58.240 align:middle line:84%
have to be exhaustive
with the pairings.

01:14:58.240 --> 01:15:00.738 align:middle line:90%
That might be why--

01:15:00.738 --> 01:15:02.530 align:middle line:84%
I think this was
Kateryna's comment earlier

01:15:02.530 --> 01:15:04.420 align:middle line:84%
that the violations
of this are not

01:15:04.420 --> 01:15:07.003 align:middle line:84%
as strong as the violations of
the head movement constraint.

01:15:07.003 --> 01:15:08.920 align:middle line:84%
And it might have to do
with things like this,

01:15:08.920 --> 01:15:11.230 align:middle line:84%
that there are extraordinary
circumstances under which

01:15:11.230 --> 01:15:14.170 align:middle line:84%
these effects might be
overwhelmed by your desire

01:15:14.170 --> 01:15:15.520 align:middle line:90%
to have--

01:15:15.520 --> 01:15:16.812 align:middle line:90%
to prioritize one or the other.

01:15:16.812 --> 01:15:18.270 align:middle line:84%
Whereas with head
movement, there's

01:15:18.270 --> 01:15:19.540 align:middle line:90%
nothing like that going on.

01:15:19.540 --> 01:15:22.803 align:middle line:90%
It's just got to move ahead.

01:15:22.803 --> 01:15:24.970 align:middle line:84%
One of the cases that people
talk about when they're

01:15:24.970 --> 01:15:26.980 align:middle line:84%
talking about
exhaustivity is cases

01:15:26.980 --> 01:15:30.740 align:middle line:84%
where you know that
there's only a single pair.

01:15:30.740 --> 01:15:37.840 align:middle line:84%
So if I'm eating lunch
with a colleague,

01:15:37.840 --> 01:15:39.940 align:middle line:84%
if I've invited a colleague
to have lunch with me

01:15:39.940 --> 01:15:41.380 align:middle line:84%
and we go and have
lunch, and then

01:15:41.380 --> 01:15:44.497 align:middle line:84%
I try to pay the
bill and my colleague

01:15:44.497 --> 01:15:46.330 align:middle line:84%
is arguing with me about
whether-- about who

01:15:46.330 --> 01:15:50.800 align:middle line:84%
gets to pay, I might say,
"Well, who invited who?"

01:15:50.800 --> 01:15:52.090 align:middle line:90%
Right?

01:15:52.090 --> 01:15:55.120 align:middle line:84%
I'm not going to say,
"Who did who invite?"

01:15:55.120 --> 01:15:56.620 align:middle line:90%
And maybe it's-- I think.

01:15:56.620 --> 01:15:59.110 align:middle line:84%
That's a case where the
judgment is pretty clear.

01:15:59.110 --> 01:16:01.960 align:middle line:84%
And I think that might
be a case where there's

01:16:01.960 --> 01:16:03.550 align:middle line:90%
no question of exhaustivity.

01:16:03.550 --> 01:16:04.930 align:middle line:84%
There are only
two people, right?

01:16:04.930 --> 01:16:07.240 align:middle line:84%
It's either I invited
you or you invited me.

01:16:07.240 --> 01:16:09.940 align:middle line:84%
Those are the only
possible answers.

01:16:09.940 --> 01:16:13.090 align:middle line:84%
And so maybe that's why
the judgment, I think,

01:16:13.090 --> 01:16:14.990 align:middle line:84%
in that kind of
case gets stronger.

01:16:14.990 --> 01:16:15.490 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:16:15.490 --> 01:16:17.350 align:middle line:84%
Sorry, did I ever manage
to answer your question

01:16:17.350 --> 01:16:17.980 align:middle line:90%
at any point of that?

01:16:17.980 --> 01:16:18.530 align:middle line:90%
Yes, good.

01:16:18.530 --> 01:16:19.030 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:16:19.030 --> 01:16:21.590 align:middle line:84%
This is all-- you're
opening a can of worms,

01:16:21.590 --> 01:16:23.020 align:middle line:90%
which I will now close.

01:16:23.020 --> 01:16:26.200 align:middle line:84%
There's lots of interesting work
on exactly this problem, yeah.

01:16:26.200 --> 01:16:28.630 align:middle line:84%
And we are just
about out of time.

01:16:28.630 --> 01:16:31.130 align:middle line:84%
Yes, we're out of time, because
this one will take too long.

01:16:31.130 --> 01:16:33.010 align:middle line:84%
So we'll do more
shortest move next time.

01:16:33.010 --> 01:16:34.330 align:middle line:90%
We'll talk more about this.

01:16:34.330 --> 01:16:34.870 align:middle line:90%
Cool.

01:16:34.870 --> 01:16:36.420 align:middle line:90%
Thanks.

01:16:36.420 --> 01:16:41.000 align:middle line:90%